Stanisław Dąmbski, Standard-bearer of Brest Kuyavia; Józef Binkowski, Steward of Gniezno; Stefan Zielonacki of Kalisz, Maciej Dąmbski of Brest Kuyavia, Deputy Judges;

Bartłomiej Kraszewski, Sub-steward of Bydgoszcz; Jan Kwilecki, Cup-bearer of Wschów; Jan Mycielski, Starost of Osiny; Piotr Drwęcki of Poznań, Rudnicki of Brest Kuyavia, Land Court Scribes;

Bojanowski, Sword-bearer of Kalisz; Stanisław Sądowski, Municipal Court Scribe of Wałcz; Umiński, Deputy Voivode of Brest Kuyavia; Stanisław Brodnicki, Land Court Surveyor of Poznań; Radoński of Kalisz, [uncertain: Stefan] Zielonacki of Kalisz, Ludwik Chmielewski of Kalisz, Boundary Surveyors;

Ksawery Małachowski of Konin, Kazimierz Gorzyński of Nakło, Court Registrars;

Stanisław Jabkowski, Starost's son of Zgierz; Ksawery Mikorski, Maciej Mierzejewski, Stanisław Trąbczyński, Ksawery Szczaniecki, Paweł Skórzewski;

which commissioners, regardless of the absence of others, when at least five of them are present, following the citations issued four weeks before the appointed date of the commission, to be signed by the first in order of rank and to be performed by those commissioners who shall not yet have been sworn to any judicial functions, shall take the oath, each at the first opportune moment, before the commencement of the commission in the nearest municipal court, by the following formula:

I, named above, swear to the Lord God Almighty, One in the Holy Trinity, that, being appointed to adjudicate the boundary dispute between the Honourable Antoni Prusimski, Starost of Niszczewice, and the Honourable Stanisław Chełmski and others party to this cause,

I shall judge this cause according to God, conscience, justice, written proofs and counter-proofs, and the testimony of examined witnesses; I shall not be led astray by any considerations, friendship, or hatred; I shall accept no gifts or promises to suppress justice; and in all my judging I shall hold God, law, and justice as the foremost purpose of my judgment — so help me God and the innocent Passion of Christ the Lord.

[Translator's note: Odwodów: the word is most naturally read as the genitive plural of odwód (counter-proof, rebuttal evidence), paired with dowodów (proofs). Translated as 'counter-proofs'. The pairing dowody i odwody is a standard legal formula in early modern Polish proceedings.]

Having come to the disputed ground, without exhausting themselves with any delay and without interruption, judging even if any party should fail to appear, they shall bring these boundaries to a final conclusion through their decision and shall resolve them definitively.

They shall weigh documents, decrees, and other boundary descriptions made by sub-chamberlains or boundary surveyors; they shall order previously conducted sworn inquiries to be submitted, and if they cannot be enlightened from the earlier ones, they shall conduct new ones; having first received the witnesses' oaths for them, they shall examine the proceedings; they shall review all decrees; they shall consider the period of use of the lands and the age of the sworn inquiries; they shall receive the oath from the party winning the boundary line, according to the prescription of the law; and they shall declare and mark such boundaries as are to endure in perpetuity.

At which time the collateral neighbours, summoned to appear under penalty of non-recovery of land should any fall to the estates being demarcated, are to present themselves. After the settlement of the boundary dispute, they shall collect the penalties imposed by the Land [court] for violent transgressions committed; they shall determine damages in excess of those of the municipal court caused by those who committed unlawful transgressions, and [the costs of] legal expenses, and shall order them to be paid by whomever it falls to.

And even if before the appointed date of the commission, or in the course of its proceedings, the present heirs of these estates should sell or cede them, or they should pass to other hands through the death of any of them — the aforementioned appointed Honourable commissioners shall, among the possessors of those estates at that time, settle, adjudicate, and conclude this cause, both as to the boundary matter and as to damages, leaving nothing unresolved, so fully that the verdict of this commission shall be law and the marking of the boundaries by them shall be permanent and perpetual.

[Andrzej Stanisław] Młodziejowski, Bishop of Poznań, Grand Chancellor of the Crown, as president, signs,

by his own hand.

Prince Adam Łodzia Poniński, Marshal of the General Confederation and of the Sejm, by his own hand.

Michał Hieronim Radziwiłł, [uncertain: Marshal, Sword-bearer], of the General Confederation of the Grand Duchy of Lithuania and of the Sejm, by his own hand.

[Translator's note: M.M.: the source transcription expands this acronym as Marszałek Miecznik?, with a question mark indicating the transcriber's uncertainty; expanded accordingly in the translation. Michał Hieronim Radziwiłł (1744–1831) held the title of Marshal of the General Confederation of the Grand Duchy of Lithuania.]

After the enrollment of the aforesaid enacted commission, inserted above, in the present records, the original of the same was returned to the same person who submitted it, in discharge of his office. Pachała, by his own hand.

[Translator's note: Orgle: the word appears in the manuscript without scribal annotation or correction mark. It does not correspond to any standard Latin term in this context; originale (the original document) is the most plausible reading and is adopted in the translation, but the word as written remains uncertain.]

[Translator's note: Pachała: the word appears immediately before manupropria in a position where a surname would be expected. It may be the name of the court official who returned the document, but the reading is uncertain.]

Place of the seal of the Royal Court of Warsaw.

Read by Grzegorzewski.

[Section 2: Proceedings of the Commission: Opening, Scribal Pen Dispute, Failed Reconciliation, Resumption of Proceedings]

Authentically extracted from the Warsaw municipal court records by extract, and thereafter filed in the Konin municipal court records on Monday, on the eve of the Feast of the Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary in the present year; and likewise on the basis and by the authority of the citation, [the citations] issued by the aforementioned Honourable commissioners, designating four weeks before the appointed date of the commission, that is, for today's date in September of the present year, and served on:

the Honourable Antoni Prusimski, Starost of Niszczewice, heir of the estates of Trąbczyn with appurtenances;

on the Reverend Heliodor, Canon of Chełmno, and Stanisław, brothers by birth of the Chełmski family, heirs of the estates of Łukomia with appurtenances;

on Ignacy Gliszczyński, Starost of Pasierby, heir of the estates of Tomice with appurtenances;

and on others party to this cause —

[the said citations having been] issued on 12 August of the present running year, dated at the Żerków Palace, signed by the Right Honourable citizen and the Honourable Ludwik Dąmbski, voivode of Brest Kuyavia, as first in order and as president of this commission by virtue of the constitution expressed above, and confirmed with his seal, filed in the Konin municipal court records on Wednesday after the Feast of the Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary, that is on 16 August of the same present year, and authentically extracted from the records for the day and appointed date expressed above:

having come to the disputed ground on 13 September, [the president] ordered his judicial jurisdiction to be established through the aforementioned Court Summoner and convened, and thereafter,

after establishing it, ordered it adjourned until Friday, that is until 15 September, on account of the Feast of the Exaltation of the Holy Cross still being observed in the Archdiocese of Gniezno; on Friday he then resumed his jurisdiction together with all the aforementioned sitting commissioners and ordered the Court Summoner to convene it; on Friday, following the public conferral by the Right Honourable and Honourable Voivode-President of this commission of the scribal pen [pióro] upon the Honourable Ludwik Chmielewski, Boundary Surveyor of Kalisz, sitting on the side of the Honourable Chełmski — the Right Honourable and Honourable Sub-chamberlain of Poznań, through the voice of the Honourable Zielonacki, a Land Court Surveyor and not a Boundary Surveyor of Kalisz, not being named in the commission instrument, having entered an objection and raised the question of the scribal pen, conferred it upon the Honourable Radoński — which conferral he maintained without regard to the presidency of the Right Honourable citizen and Honourable Voivode-President, together with the other commissioners sitting on the side of the Honourable Chełmski — and so further proceedings were blocked by this dispute on that day; in this situation, and also to allow the parties to prepare for the court, the jurisdiction was adjourned to Saturday with the unanimous consent of all the Right Honourable and Honourable Commissioners.

[Translator's note: Pióro: the dispute here is a dispute over which party's representative would produce the official account of the commission. See Glossary: scribal pen.]

On Saturday, that is 16 September: since the Right Honourable and Honourable Voivode of Brest Kuyavia, the president, had fallen ill, and in addition the heavy rain falling all day did not permit going out to the field, the Right Honourable and Honourable President wrote a letter to the Right Honourable and Honourable Sub-chamberlain and to his colleagues informing them that for these reasons it was not possible to go out to the field that day, and at the same time requesting their consent to adjourn the jurisdiction — Sunday intervening — until Monday; and to adjourn this jurisdiction he sent the Honourable Piotr Rajewicz, advocate for the cause of the Honourable Starost of Niszczewice, with two commissioners to the place of the dispute that afternoon, and ordered this jurisdiction dissolved until Monday.

The next day, on Sunday, after this adjourned jurisdiction and the adjournment made until Monday: on that Sunday, the Right Honourable and Honourable Sub-chamberlain, together with his colleagues, and requested by the side [of Chełmski], having come to the Honourable Starost of Niszczewice and arrived at Trąbczyn, saying nothing of what he might have had to do on Saturday

in the field — since on account of the rain falling all day he could not have done anything either — and being in good harmony, on the reconciliation proposed to both parties by the whole court, the president himself requested and delegated two of the Right Honourable, Reverend, and Honourable Commissioners on the side of the Honourable Chełmski, and two others on the side of the Honourable Prusimski.

[Translator's note: The Sunday visit and reconciliation attempt: No judicial proceedings could lawfully take place on Sunday (cf. interveniente die Dominico, above). The Sub-chamberlain's visit to Prusimski at Trąbczyn was therefore informal and conciliatory in character. The Sub-chamberlain (Michał Skorzewski, Poznań Sub-chamberlain) had been sitting on Chełmski's side throughout the proceedings, having aligned himself with that faction in the scribal pen dispute on the Friday; it was accordingly Chełmski's side that invited him to make this visit. The document notes pointedly that no one raised the question of Prusimski's non-appearance in the field on Saturday — implicitly because the rain had prevented any field activity regardless. The four commissioners then delegated to attempt reconciliation were drawn two from each side: the Sub-chamberlain's faction nominated two from Chełmski's side, and the President nominated two from Prusimski's side. This two-day attempt (Monday and Tuesday) failed, and proceedings resumed judicially on Wednesday. The Chełmski faction then claimed that Prusimski had been in legal default (contumax) for Saturday's non-appearance — a claim whose credibility is quietly undermined by this passage's account of the Sunday meeting, at which no such objection was raised.]

And so the Right Honourable, Reverend, and Honourable delegated Commissioners, wishing to reconcile the parties, negotiating this reconciliation for two days, that is through Monday and Tuesday, by making representations and showing the places and names of the forests — when they could not reconcile the parties, the whole substance of the matter turned to litigation; and so, after this failed reconciliation, the Right Honourable and Honourable Voivode-President, together with his colleagues, proceeding judicially, on Wednesday came to the disputed ground, to the place where the whole court sat with him, and ordered the judicial jurisdiction to be resumed and convened, and invited the Right Honourable citizen and Honourable Sub-chamberlain together with his colleagues to join him; finally, settling the dispute over the scribal pen, and not wishing to be an occasion for further quarrel and the splitting of the court, he declared through the two Right Honourable and Honourable delegated commissioners that — upon the Honourable Chmielewski's relinquishing the pen, which the Honourable Chmielewski declared he would do — he would give that same pen to the Honourable Radoński, upon whom it had been conferred; but the Honourable Chełmski, and together with him those judging on his side, the Most Honourable lords and the Honourable loyal Commissioners, saying that since the Honourable Starost of Niszczewice had not been in the field on Saturday he was therefore in default, and accordingly they would judge him as one in default, and that they had no reason to join [the president's court] — and so, having removed themselves from the place of the jointly established jurisdiction, not wishing to join and indeed having split away, they proceeded in the split court to their further proceedings.

[Translator's note: Chmielewski and Radoński: Both were Kalisz Boundary Surveyors named in the commission. Chmielewski (Ludwik Chmielewski) had been sitting on Chełmski's side, and was the President's original choice for the scribal pen. Radoński (Tomasz Radoński) was also a Kalisz Boundary Surveyor sitting on Chełmski's side, and was the Sub-chamberlain's counter-choice. The dispute was therefore within the Chełmski faction itself: the President had conferred the pen on Chmielewski; the Sub-chamberlain overrode this and insisted on Radoński. The President's proposed resolution was a face-saving compromise — Chmielewski would formally relinquish the pen, allowing it to pass to the Sub-chamberlain's choice — but Chełmski's faction rejected even this and proceeded to split the court.]

[Translator's note: "urodzeni uczciwi Komisarze": uczciwi (honest, trustworthy, loyal) is here used as a plain adjective modifying Komisarze, applied to persons already designated urodzeni (noble). It does not function as the sub-noble honorific uczciwy (Rule 5). Rendered as "loyal".]

And so likewise, in response to this split on the part of the Right Honourable and Honourable Sub-chamberlain and his commissioners, the Right Honourable and Honourable President, with five commissioners named below in the signatures, acting in accordance with the constitution — which had entrusted and ordered that, without regard to the number of commissioners, so long as five are present in number, even notwithstanding the default of any party, these causes be fully and finally adjudicated — proceeded to mutual judgment.

[Section 3: Citations, Appearances, and Document Production]

And so, on the basis of this constitution — which is to be observed in all things and not interpreted — and likewise on the basis of the citations expressed above, and of the appointed dates [served] by both parties for the said commission, or rather constitution and citations expressed above — and indeed [served] by the Honourable Prusimski, Starost of Niszczewice, five [citations]:

the first, against the Honourable Heliodor, Canon of Chełmno, and Stanisław, brothers by birth of the Chełmski family, heirs of the estates of Łukomia and others;

the second, against the Most Reverend Konstanty Jełowiecki, Abbot, and his entire Ląd Convent, heirs of the estates of Drzewce and of the town of Zagórów; and likewise against the Honourable Ignacy Gałecki, Starost of Bydgoszcz, heir of the estates of Biskupice and the deserted lands of Bystrzyce; Helena Wężyk, widow of the late Honourable Józef Wężyk of Osin, Castellan of Sieradz; Franciszek and Barbara Stadniccy of the Starosty of Ostrzeszów; Paweł and Konstancja Skorzewscy, successors of the said late Honourable Wężyk, heirs of the estates of Królikowo, Groja and others; Franciszka Zakrzewska, wife of the Land Judge of Kalisz, as mother, and Jan Nepomucen Zakrzewski, her son, heirs of the estates of Podbiel and part of Szetlew; Maciej Jan Jaroszewski, and likewise the surviving [heir] and successors of the late lamented Fulwarski, co-heirs and possessors of parts of [those] estates — in respect of the demarcation of their estates from his estates, or the showing of corner mounds adjoining his estates, and likewise in the matter of the production of documents and sworn inquiries previously conducted — issued in the Konin municipal court on Wednesday after the Feast of the Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary, under one act, that is on 16 August, extracted authentically from the records;

the third, against the aforementioned Honourable Gliszczyński, father and son, heirs of the estates of Tomice — issued in the Pyzdry municipal court on the Wednesday following the same feast, that is likewise on 16 August of the present year, and thereafter filed in the Kalisz municipal court on Tuesday after Saint Bartholomew, that is on 20 August;

the fourth, against the surviving [heir] and successors of the late Honourable Ignacy Gliszczyński, Starost of Pasierby, reviving a lapsed cause after his death — likewise issued in the same Kalisz municipal court by the same person on the same day and year;

the fifth, against the heirs and possessors of the estates of Kurowo and Kurówek, in the same matter — issued in the Pyzdry municipal court on Monday after the Feast of the Nativity of the Blessed Virgin Mary, that is on 11 September

of the same year.

And on the side of the Honourable Stanisław Chełmski, heir of the estates of Łukomia with appurtenances, against the Honourable Antoni Prusimski, Starost of Niszczewice, heir of the estates of Trąbczyn Wielki and Mały or Nowa Wieś and others, and against the Most Reverend Konstanty Jełowiecki, Abbot, and his Ląd Convent, heirs of the estates of Drzewce, [and against] Ignacy Gałecki, Starost of Bydgoszcz, heir of the estates of Biskupice and the deserted lands of Bystrzyce — likewise issued in the Konin municipal court on Wednesday after the Feast of the Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary, that is on 16 August of the same year —

[the commission] orders all these parties to appear and file their court appearances.

[Translator's note: Party-initiated citations: The citations were not issued by the commission on its own motion but were initiated by the parties themselves, each going to the relevant municipal court to have the instruments entered in the records. Prusimski filed five citations covering a wide range of collateral neighbours and co-heirs; Chełmski filed one, naming Prusimski, the Ląd Convent (Jełowiecki), and Gałecki. The commission's role was to act on those citations by ordering the named parties to appear. This was standard early modern Polish procedure: a party initiated the citation, the court recorded and issued it, and the commission enforced appearance on that basis.]

And since the Honourable Antoni Prusimski, Starost of Niszczewice, heir of the estates of Trąbczyn Mały or Nowa Wieś, Osiny, Łazy, Szetlew and the olędry of Osiny, appeared personally, as plaintiff and defendant; the Honourables Walenty Wardęski, Stanisław Jaroszewski, Wojciech Wardęski, Sebastian Krzyżkowski, Kasper Kurowski, co-heirs and possessors of their portions of the village of Kurowo Wielki, appeared personally as defendants; the Honourable Maci Jaroszewski, son of the late Honourable Walenty, Jan Jaroszewski, son of the late Honourable Stanisław Jaroszewski, and likewise Maci Jaroszewski, Jan's own nephew and son of the late Paweł, and also the Honourable Urszula née Sławińska, widow of the late Honourable Piotr Fulwarski, in her own name and in the name of her children, co-heirs and possessors of their portions of the manorial village of Szetlew, appeared personally as defendants; the Honourable Józef Pawłowski, summoned personally for the giving of testimony, appeared — all of whom the court of the dispute having heard, and since not only the Crown law requires that collateral neighbours on the boundary be summoned, but the constitution or commission enacted by the Estates likewise recommends the same:

this court therefore orders the Honourable Prusimski, satisfying both the law of the Crown and the enacted constitution, to submit and produce in court the collateral citations of his corner neighbours and collateral parties. The Honourable Prusimski, complying with the orders of his court, submitted the aforementioned citations, issued both against the principal parties bordering his estates and against the collateral parties; and upon their production and compliance with

the sentence of his court, the court declares him free in this respect, and orders the parties to proceed further with their pleadings. In the further pleading of the parties, the court of the dispute hearing them and proceeding in accordance with the terms of the constitution and the appointed dates, finds that for more thorough information and the showing of the boundaries and the corner mound, it is necessary to submit all documents concerning the boundaries and corner mounds of the aforementioned estates, and likewise the sworn inquiries conducted both in respect of the corner mounds at the past field inspections and in respect of the transgressions committed in former times and of the claimed stawisko, and particularly in the year 1728 — as also specified in the Tribunal decrees and the present commission for submission — and orders the Honourable Prusimski to submit, without delay and under oath in his court, all documents relating to the present boundary cause, together with the sworn inquiries mentioned above, and likewise the collateral neighbours [to submit theirs].

The parties complying with the orders of their court: the Honourable Prusimski submitted both the paired sworn inquiries in sealed rolls and the documents listed in a schedule signed by his court [representative], and also others beyond the schedule contained in bundles, and offers to swear to them. The Honourable Maci Jaroszewski likewise submitted a copy of the boundaries between the estates of Szetlew and Szetlewek of the year 1581. The Honourable Urszula Fulwarska likewise submitted a copy of the partition among the Trąbczyński family of the estates of Trąbczyn, Trąbczyn Mały or Nowa Wieś, Osiny, Łazy and others, effected in 1693, to be found in the Pyzdry municipal court — which [documents] the Most Honourable and Honourable President signed at the present commission — and they offer to swear that they have no more. The other co-heirs of the estates of Szetlew and Kurowo declare under oath that they have no documents relating to the present boundary cause nor can they submit any. The parties present in their court appearance, and in particular the heir of Trąbczyn, not content with this production, demand from one another a fuller production before the oath is sworn.

This court therefore, having suspended the oath over the submitted documents, orders the parties named above appearing in the court appearance to produce their documents more fully. And since the parties, instead of a fuller production, declare under oath that they have no further documents nor can they produce them, and prepare to swear honestly to the documents [already submitted], this court, resolving the suspended oath, orders that these oaths be performed, with this formula:

and indeed by the Honourable Antoni Prusimski, Starost of Niszczewice, Maci Jaroszewski, and Urszula Fulwarska, with the following formula: that they have produced honestly and justly all documents relating to the boundary cause of their estates and have not concealed any to the detriment and harm of their neighbour. And the Honourables and the Honest Walenty Wardęski, Stanisław Jaroszewski, Wojciech Wardęski, Sebastian Krzyżkowski, Kasper Kurowski, co-heirs of Kurowo Wielki: that they have no documents relating to the present boundary cause of those estates, nor do they know of any, nor do they conceal any. To the performance of this oath and the announcement of its formula the Court Summoner was assigned. All performed the oath with the formula expressed above. After the performance of this oath, the court, having released the parties from further production of documents, orders the same parties to continue with their pleadings — without regard to the non-appearance of the Honourable Chełmski, his brother, and others — by virtue of the constitution. In the further pleading of the parties, having heard their dispute, and having suspended for the time being the unsealing and reading of the sworn inquiries submitted to its court — the better to take note of and to establish the places and names mentioned in the sworn inquiries and documents, and the better to identify the corner mounds formed differently by each party, and finally to establish whether supplementary sworn inquiries will be needed — the court orders the Honourable Prusimski, Starost of Niszczewice, heir of the estates of Trąbczyn Wielki and Mały or Nowa Wieś, Osiny, Łazy, and Szetlewek, to show and walk out his boundary line between his estates of Trąbczyn Mały or Nowa Wieś and others, and the inherited estates of Łukomia with appurtenances of the Honourable Chełmski, for execution — to the execution of which the court offers to proceed.

[Section 4: The Boundary Line — Prusimski's Case]

The Honourable Prusimski, complying with the orders of his court, having proceeded to the execution of his boundary line and establishing his starting point, began that boundary line between these estates and Łukomia

and the other inherited estates of the Honourable Chełmski, from the place and boundary mounds described in the [commission] act, where having set his court, asserting that here, here in this place and nowhere else, is the confluence of the grounds of three estates, namely Łukomia, Drzewce, and Trąbczyn Mały or Nowa Wieś. Confirming this confluence, he showed first the Łukomia pine forest and woodland, lying to the west, to the right beyond the beaten road running from Zagórów and Drzewce, belonging and used without any dispute by Łukomia; his own Trąbczyn woodland, lying to the south on the other side of that road, called Ostrowce in this place, belonging to Trąbczyn from of old and in current use, but contested by Łukom; and the Drzewce pine forest lying to the north, not in dispute, used by Drzewce up to this point now and formerly — thereby demonstrating the evident confluence, that is the convergence of these three estates. To further confirm this convergence he showed two boundary mounds: one well-marked and evident; the second not so very marked but having the roundness and likeness of a mound and a small ditch around it, that is a crown; and a third low mound, not very marked, which he asserted to be a damaged one — lying a few paces from one another, in a triangle, in this place between the roads described in the act, to be found there and recorded in the past field acts.

And further, testing the position, that is the position of these corner mounds, as expressed and described in the judicial site inspection of 1592, known from the road that leads to Pyzdry between the estates of Trąbczyn Mały, Osiny, Łukomia, and the properties of the Ląd Convent — he showed first of all the old road, sufficiently still marked and deep, overgrown, lying a good half-staj before the mounds, described in the present act, asserting that this is that road, not the road from Osiny, and that it is the ancient Pyzdry road, properly running between the estates of Trąbczyn Mały, Osiny, Łukomia, and the properties of the Ląd Convent, that is Drzewce, Zagórów, and Wrąbczyn, all the way to Pyzdry itself; whereas the road from Osiny now running to Pyzdry via Łukom came into use only when that other road was blocked up and abandoned, that is, changed from the Osiny road to the Pyzdry road — but nevertheless this Osiny road too branches off from that same old Pyzdry road above [i.e., to the north] before these corner mounds, and then rejoins the same road beyond Łukomia — which he also showed to the court.

And further, testing also what the people say — that these corner mounds should be and lie beside the road from Drzewce going towards Łukomia — the Honourable Prusimski says that that same Zagórów road passing through Drzewce is at the same time the Drzewce road, because taking it from the closer village of Drzewce it can be called nothing but the Drzewce road; and he immediately shows also another old road, still sufficiently marked, branching off to the right from the second, the Zagórów road and at the same time the Drzewce road, emerging close to the corner mounds beneath the hill alongside which runs the road from Osiny to Pyzdry by a shortcut, joining the present Pyzdry road and running together with the Osiny road towards Pyzdry and towards Łukom — showing precisely the position of the corner mounds, as the people too say.

And further, testing the assertion that this third mound was scattered by the former heirs of Łukomia, he submits a manifest of 1719, on Tuesday after the Sunday called Reminiscere, in the Konin municipal court, concerning the scattering of the corner mound dividing the estate of Trąbczyn, Łomowo belonging to the principal village of Łukomia, and Drzewce; and likewise the judicial site inspection of 1726, on Tuesday after Saint Francis the Confessor, conducted officially in the Pyzdry municipal court concerning the dispersal and demolition of the Trąbczyn mound; and further the manifest entered under the act of that same judicial site inspection, concerning a similar dispersal and ploughing up of the mounds by the late Honourable Krzysztof Prusimski, his grandfather, heir of Trąbczyn with appurtenances, against the former heirs of Łukomia, predecessors of the Honourable Chełmski — and moreover he additionally submits a manifest and invokes a like proceeding, that is concerning the damage to one mound and the private removal of another in the Grądzeń boundary with the Ląd Convent, lodged in the Konin municipal court on Friday after Low Sunday of 1733 against the predecessors of the Honourable Chełmski, heirs of Łukomia — and this same mound the Honourable Prusimski now protesting declares to have been actual formerly and thereafter damaged.

[Translator's note: Prywatnie usucie: asterisked in the source, indicating an uncertain reading in the transcription. The phrase most likely means the private removal or pulling-out of a mound. Translated accordingly.]

And further, proving that the Honourable Chełmski himself acknowledged these corner mounds at the past three field sessions, and moreover together with his brother acknowledged them as corner mounds — with only the difference that he held and acknowledged them to be not between Trąbczyn, Drzewce, and Łukomia as

the Convent and the Honourable Prusimski maintained and acknowledged, but between Łukomia, Bukowe, and Drzewce — he submits three field decrees: the first of 1759, adjudicated in the field between these parties by two Sieradz Boundary Surveyors, filed in the Pyzdry municipal court on Thursday after the Sunday called Cantate in the same year; the second of 1760, made in the field on the Monday after the Sunday called Exaudi by the Łęczyca Boundary Surveyor in the Drzewce boundary with Trąbczyn, filed in the Konin municipal court on Friday on the very feast of Saint Anthony of Padua of the same year; the third established by the Łęczyca and Gostyń Boundary Surveyors on the Monday after Saint Bartholomew of 1760, filed in the Konin municipal court on the Monday after the Sunday called Jubilate of 1761.

And to further prove that here is the true corner mound and boundary mounds — the better to verify them — he showed thirty visible mounds of the same form and size as the first prominent one in the corner mound, lying in the boundary between Drzewce and Trąbczyn without great difficulty of identification, and described from these corner mounds to the second corner mound and confluence of the villages of Skokum, Drzewce Convent, and Trąbczyn inherited by the Honourable Prusimski — set up by the field decree of 1760 by the Łęczyca Boundary Surveyor, expressed above from the Oblata.

In this same showing, the Honourable Prusimski also remonstrates to his court that the Honourable Chełmski — forming for himself both at the past three field sessions and now a boundary as though between Drzewce and Łukomia, and as though from that corner mound, formed by the Honourable Prusimski before and now, and consistently acknowledged at all times during the three field sessions by the Convent, which the serfs from Drzewce present here below named now also acknowledge — stretching to a second supposed corner mound at the head, that is at the beginning, of the woodland called Ostrowce, near the Osiny road itself, established by him, through fourteen mounds only:

not only takes for himself and appropriates, from the aforementioned thirty boundary mounds of Drzewce and Trąbczyn, fourteen for his invented boundary; but also, converting one of these fourteen mounds and then again a fifteenth Drzewce boundary mound — although lying progressively in one line at a distance from one another — into corner mounds, and taking a third overturned stump still further from them as a corner mound, forms and establishes for himself from such mounds a corner mound at the head of the Ostrowce woodland near the Osiny road itself, as though lying and existing between Łukomia, Drzewce, and Trąbczyn; and by this corner mound takes from Trąbczyn the entire Ostrowce woodland, as the heirs and people of Trąbczyn maintain it is called and has always belonged to Trąbczyn.

This woodland, together with the pine forest, beginning from there, that is from the Honourable Chełmski's corner mound and reaching as far as the Honourable Prusimski's corner mound and to the road running from Zagórów and Drzewce to Kalisz, Chocz, and Tomice, the people assert has belonged to Trąbczyn from of old. And as it is properly Trąbczyn's, the Honourable Prusimski submits in support the judicial site inspection in the Pyzdry municipal court of 1644, on Monday after the Sunday called Rogationum, conducted on behalf of the late Honourables Marcin, Wojciech, Władysław, and Andrzej Trąbczyński, heirs of Trąbczyn, in the Ostrowce woodland belonging to Trąbczyn, concerning eight fresh blazes made on pine trees and one hundred and fifty trees of various kinds cut during hunting for stakes for nets, against the late Honourable Jerzy Chełmski, heir of Łukomia.

And likewise the proceedings relating to that judicial site inspection, that is two default decrees: the first concerning the hunting and killing of game in the Trąbczyn woodland; the second concerning the cutting of timber for stakes for nets to the number of one hundred and fifty pieces, and likewise for other purposes to the number of one hundred pieces, also in the Trąbczyn woodland — obtained on behalf of the above-named Honourables Trąbczyński and Anna née Słończyc, widow of the late Stanisław Trąbczyński, against the late Honourable Jerzy Chełmski, heir of Łukomia, in the Konin land court in 1644 on Monday after the Octave of Corpus Christi, under one act.

And likewise two Konin land court decrees in the same year between these parties, on Monday after Saint Hedwig, with motions to the Tribunal admitted, and two Piotrków Tribunal decrees of deferral in this cause on both counts against the same Honourable Chełmski, issued in 1645 on Tuesday before the Feast of Saint Prisca from the aforementioned motions, and made.

And moreover two further judicial site inspections in respect of that same Ostrowce woodland, with its name expressly mentioned and its belonging to Trąbczyn stated among other woodlands: the first of 1724, on Wednesday after Saint Prisca, in the Konin municipal court; and the second in the Pyzdry municipal court after Saint Catherine of 1725 — both conducted on behalf of and at the suit of the late Honourable Krzysztof Prusimski, heir of Trąbczyn and grandfather of the Honourable Starost of Niszczewice, against the late Honourable Józef Chełmski, heir of Łukomia — and likewise the proceedings conducted in this matter, expressed in the schedule, he submits.

And finally, proving that Bukowe — whether deserted lands or merely a name for ground lying within the Łukomia boundaries — has no convergence of any kind and no separate boundaries, and cannot have a corner mound here in the place where the Honourable [Prusimski] now and formerly, and likewise the Ląd Convent together with him, established their corner mound — and that here the convergence and corner mound of Łukomia, Drzewce, and Trąbczyn as shown by the Honourable Prusimski is and must be the actual one — and likewise proving the former demarcation of Trąbczyn with Drzewce, he submits not only the above-mentioned field decree of 1760, made from sworn inquiries and documents expressed above from the Oblata, which sets out Bukowe as ground lying only within the Łukomia boundaries having no convergence nor separate boundaries, not touching this place here, and situated and found near Imielno — but also the documents expressed below relating to this circumstance and to this corner mound.

And firstly from those, as to the Drzewce boundary with Trąbczyn or Nowa Wieś, he submits a deed in the prescribed order for a compromise between the heir of Trąbczyn and the Ląd Convent concerning the demarcation of the estates of Trąbczyn Wielki and Mały with the Convent villages, of 1,443, on Monday the day after the Sunday called Reminiscere, attested in the Konin land court records and filed in the Poznań municipal court on Friday after Saint Michael of 1761 —

asserting that according to this compromise, or some other act which cannot be found on account of the lapse of time, the mounds in the Drzewce and Trąbczyn boundary, set up from the Łukomia corner mound to this corner mound here, still sufficiently marked, of which there are thirty, must have been heaped.

[Translator's note: Ob revolutionem temporum: literally "on account of the revolution of the times", a standard Latin legal formula meaning that the document in question has been lost or cannot be produced due to the passage of time.]

And in the later period, as to the second, the Łukomia boundary with Trąbczyn, he submits two default decrees:

the first concerning the Łukomia boundary, obtained against the late Honourable Jerzy Chełmski and others, co-heirs of the jointly held villages or estates of Łukomia, Imielno, and Łomowo — as directly expressed in this default — concerning the demarcation of the said estates of Łukomia, Imielno, and Łomowo, lying in one ground, from Trąbczyn and other villages, the principal defendants;

the second against the Ląd Convent, heirs of the village of Drzewce, summoned collaterally likewise concerning the demarcation of Drzewce from Trąbczyn, for the same appointed date, at the suit and action of the heirs of Trąbczyn with appurtenances, namely the aforementioned Trąbczyński family and Dybryński —

Both obtained in the Konin land court in 1644 on Monday after the Octave of Corpus Christi, under one act, without the slightest mention or reference to Bukowe.

And likewise two decrees of motions, similarly concerning the demarcation of those estates between the same parties — and indeed with the heirs of Łukomia, Imielno, and Łomowo, with the same expression that Łukom, Imielno, and Łomowo lie in one ground — likewise without any mention whatsoever of Bukowe, issued in the same Konin land court in the same year on Monday after Saint Hedwig, after the above-mentioned default.

And moreover, to confirm his corner mound, the Honourable Prusimski submits the compromise decree between the present Honourable Chełmski, heir of Łukomia with other appurtenances, and the Ląd Convent, of 1761, on Thursday after the Sunday called Misericordiae, made in the field, after the above-mentioned field decree of 1760 —

acknowledging no convergence nor any Bukowe boundary, but only setting out the ground called Bukowe as lying within the Łukomia boundaries, and showing moreover that neither Imielno nor Bukowe are bounded off from Łukomia of itself, but lie within the body of the Łukomia boundaries — filed in the Kalisz municipal court on the Monday after the Sunday Cantate.

By this decree, though made without the court appearance of the Honourable Prusimski, the Honourable Prusimski tests and proves that the Honourable Chełmski, in running out his boundary with Drzewce, made no mention either of a corner mound or of the boundary itself, and moreover began his Łukomia — not Bukowe — boundary with Drzewce from the Zagórów corner mound, and ran and established it between Łukomia and Drzewce — not between Bukowe — and determined it no further than a staj before the Honourable Prusimski's corner mound, directing it straight into that corner mound, and allowed the compromise court to mark it with mounds only up to that point. And by this his own boundary he confirmed and acknowledged not only the Łukomia — not Bukowe — boundary with Drzewce, but also the corner mound of the Honourable Prusimski and the convergence of Trąbczyn or Nowa Wieś. For these two boundaries of Łukomia with Drzewce, having no other third estate before them — especially being determined so close before the corner mound — can only reach Trąbczyn or Nowa Wieś as their third, and must necessarily share the same convergence and the same corner mound together, and must necessarily end and be determined thus and in no other way at the corner mound formed by the Honourable Prusimski, and likewise by the Ląd Convent, during the three field sessions and now shown.

It follows therefore that Bukowe cannot be in this place and at the corner mound of the Honourable Prusimski and the Ląd Convent, and cannot have any convergence of its own there; nor is there or can there be a corner mound of Bukowe, Łukomia, and Drzewce in this place, as the Honourable Chełmski alleged at the three sessions

and now alleges — but the true and genuine corner mound of Łukomia, Drzewce, and Trąbczyn or Nowa Wieś is here, as the Honourable Prusimski and the Convent established it at the three field sessions, and as the Honourable Starost now establishes and shows it. And since therefore the true corner mound of Łukomia, Drzewce, and Trąbczyn is here, and the Honourable Chełmski vacillates in his allegation of the corner mound — both his corner mounds, the one which he places at the starting point at the head of the Ostrowce woodland right by the Osiny road, also against the judicial site inspection of 1592, and the one which he places at the terminal point as though by reason of the convergence of Bukowe at the corner mound of the Honourable Starost, putting Bukowe in place of Trąbczyn and pushing Trąbczyn aside — and likewise the entire boundary of the Honourable Chełmski through thirteen Drzewce and Trąbczyn boundary mounds as though forming a Łukomia boundary with Drzewce between these two corner mounds — are wrongly and unlawfully invented.

The Honourable Prusimski says and proves that his own corner mound is good and lawful. And further, still asserting this corner mound and calling to his court's memory the three above-mentioned field decrees, the Honourable Prusimski says: although the Convent is not now present in writing here at this act, and does not appear itself but only the Drzewce serfs —

nevertheless this same corner mound and confluence of Łukomia, Drzewce, and Trąbczyn — not Bukowe — during the three field sessions held

with the Honourable Chełmski, it unfailingly acknowledged in this place together with him, and moreover the Convent itself, as to the Drzewce boundary with Łukomia, established and ran it from the Zagórów corner mound together with the Honourable Chełmski all the way to here, and proved this with a sworn inquiry — which the same Convent's serfs from Drzewce and from Trąbczyn, named above in the boundary line account, now acknowledge and maintain. And moreover the Convent, not acknowledging either of the corner mounds nor the boundary of the Honourable Chełmski, and never acknowledging any convergence of Bukowe either — for all of this protested always together with the Honourable Prusimski, as their protests recorded in the field decrees attest, against the Honourable Chełmski — consistently maintained and acknowledged this corner mound. Which corner mound also now, together with the roads, the people from Drzewce present here, in the absence of their lords —

as likewise from Trąbczyn, ready to acknowledge it when questioned by their court — namely from Drzewce: the labourers Tomasz and Kazimierz Czerniak, Tomasz and Fabian, Stefan Głąbik the juror, Jan Kuwal, Kazimierz Dąbek the wheelwright; from Trąbczyn: Szyman Boderek aged ninety-nine, Bartłomiej the Court Summoner, Maci Wąchnicki the forester, Piotr Szyman, Nadolnik, Wojciech Zelak — and likewise the Honourable Józef Pawłowski, a nobleman raised here in the neighbourhood and serving with the predecessors of the Honourable Chełmski — showing the pine forests and woodlands of each estate separately, namely: the Łukomia pine forest lying to the west, to the right of the Zagórów road and at the same time the Drzewce road, belonging to Łukomia from of old and thus long called and named; and the Ostrowce woodland with pine forest

so called, the Trąbczyn [woodland], lying to the south on the other side of that same road, belonging to Trąbczyn from of old and in current use, now contested, touching the corner mounds; and the Drzewce pine forest lying to the north, touching the Ostrowce woodland, marked by mounds still beyond the Osiny road — taken by the Honourable Chełmski into his boundary — belonging to and used by Drzewce from of old up to those mounds: acknowledging not only the confluence but also the corner mound together with the three mounds of these three estates of Łukomia, Drzewce, and Trąbczyn or Nowa Wieś — keeping their corner mound albeit in the absence of their lords — and not acknowledging any corner mound or boundary of Bukowe. And likewise the old road shown by the Honourable Prusimski lying several dozen paces before the corner mounds, being well marked and beaten, they acknowledge as the old Pyzdry road, and say and acknowledge that from Osiny, Trąbczyn, and other villages they have gone past Łukomia of the Honourable Chełmski, Drzewce, Zagórów, and Wrąbczyn — the Convent villages — to Pyzdry from of old.

And the Osiny road, now turned towards Pyzdry via Łukom not so very long ago — and that this Osiny road branches off further above [i.e., to the north] from that same old Pyzdry road before the corner mounds towards Osiny, and afterwards rejoins the same road again beyond Łukomia — and likewise

the Zagórów road when one goes from Drzewce — and the Drzewce road branching from it — they name and acknowledge. And likewise they acknowledge the Ostrowce woodland: that it is here in this place, and not another, and has been called thus from of old.

The Honourable Prusimski having thus submitted these proofs at the starting point and demonstrated the truth and substance of his [corner mound], having proved this here at once, first protests against the Honourable Chełmski, heir of Łukomia with appurtenances: that although he well knows that in this place there is the true confluence of the grounds and pine forests of three estates, namely Łukomia, Drzewce, and Trąbczyn or Nowa Wieś, and that from this very place these estates have used and encompassed the woodland and pine forests, and that the Ostrowce woodland with pine forest belonging to Trąbczyn from of old and in current use reaches to this place, and that accordingly in this place the true corner mound is here and the three mounds are truly corner mounds — which he himself acknowledged at the three field sessions, with only the difference that he judged Bukowe in place of Trąbczyn — whereas the heirs of Drzewce and Trąbczyn, pushing back the estate of Bukowe as having no separate boundaries but lying within the boundaries of Łukomia, acknowledged them as not of Bukowe but only of Łukomia, Drzewce, and Trąbczyn —

and finally he himself acknowledged and confirmed the same convergence and his own boundary, run from the Zagórów corner mound between Łukomia and Drzewce — not between Bukowe — during the compromise with the Ląd Convent, and determined to stop only a staj before this corner mound, directed straight towards it — and thereby not only the convergence and his alleged corner mound both

as placed at the starting point at the head of the Ostrowce woodland and at the terminal point, but also his own boundary through thirteen mounds as though formed between Drzewce and Łukomia by himself — he utterly destroyed.

And likewise that he wrongly and unlawfully forms and establishes for himself both these corner mounds — both at the starting point and terminal point — and between them as though a Łukomia boundary with Drzewce; and moreover that even according to the judicial site inspection of 1592, which the Honourable Chełmski takes as his greatest foundation for the present cause, there is and is to be found the old Pyzdry road, still sufficiently marked, beaten and deep, lying a few lasek above towards Drzewce before these mounds, which the people also remember and as expressed above have acknowledged — and that this same road, as the judicial site inspection records, runs between the estates of Trąbczyn Mały, Osiny, Łukomia, and the properties of the Ląd Convent, that is Drzewce, Zagórów, and Wrąbczyn belonging to Ląd, all the way to Pyzdry; and that from this old road the present road by which one now travels from Osiny, Trąbczyn Mały and other estates to Pyzdry branches off not far before these mounds towards Osiny, and afterwards rejoins that same old road again beyond Łukomia, and from that junction continues all the way to Pyzdry itself —

nevertheless, notwithstanding all this, to the diminishment and harm of the grounds and pine forests of the heir of the estates of Trąbczyn Mały or Nowa Wieś, the said Honourable Chełmski, acting wrongly and unjustly, at the head, that is at the beginning, of the said Ostrowce woodland belonging to Trąbczyn from of old — as the judicial site inspection of 1644, on Monday after the Sunday of the Cross, conducted by Wojciech, Władysław, and Andrzej Trąbczyński, heirs of Trąbczyn, and another later one by Krzysztof Prusimski, heirs of Trąbczyn, against the heirs of Łukomia, and the proceedings therein, attest —

establishes and forms for himself a corner mound on the proper ground of Trąbczyn Mały, and at the same time another as though for Bukowe, Łukomia, and Drzewce, in the place of the corner mound of the Honourable Prusimski and the Ląd Convent, taking two Drzewce and Trąbczyn boundary mounds and a third overturned stump of wood — not in a triangle but lying far from one another; and in his boundary from the supposed Bukowe corner mound to his second corner mound at the head of the Ostrowce woodland he takes and turns thirteen Drzewce and Trąbczyn boundary mounds; and thereby wrongly intends to appropriate and is appropriating the ground and Ostrowce woodland, and further the marshes or stawisko, and likewise the woodlands Miąsykąt, Dziadulina and others — all proper Trąbczyn property.

Then secondly against the Ląd Convent, and more particularly against the Reverend Father Sojecki their plenipotentiary: that the said Convent, and more particularly their plenipotentiary, notwithstanding that they always at the past several field sessions consistently acknowledged the corner mound of Łukomia, Drzewce, and Trąbczyn — not Bukowe — in this same place where the Honourable Prusimski now establishes it, or rather they themselves established it as to the Drzewce boundary with Łukomia from the Zagórów corner mound together with the Honourable Chełmski all the way to here, and proved it by sworn inquiry — which the same Convent's serfs from Drzewce, and from Trąbczyn named above in the boundary line account, now acknowledge and maintain —

nevertheless now making some pretence of doubt regarding their acknowledgement, and thinking as one with the Honourable Chełmski, they are now joining him, and having taken a lead from the Honourable Chełmski against the protesting party, and not blocking the corner mound to the diminishment of the ground of the complainant — which he established at the head of the Ostrowce woodland, and which they never acknowledged before and against which they protested, and indeed contrary to their own acknowledgement and recognition

as clergy, and contrary to all justice and the proofs — acting in collusion with the Honourable Chełmski to the ruin and wrong of the complainant, and wrongly revoking their recognition as though made through ignorance of the documents which they had then and now — to the scandal of secular persons — acknowledge that corner mound to the Honourable Chełmski, do not contest or block it, and thereby both the said Convent and the Honourable Chełmski intend and are preparing to appropriate the complainant's proper ground and to deprive him of it to his detriment.

And therefore by this their acknowledgement and recognition contrary to their former position they are giving cause — as it were — for greater dispute and costs to the parties (which as ecclesiastics they ought above all to avoid, and are doing so perhaps with the purpose, as noted above, of the agreement during the compromise made between them concerning the boundaries, and now evaded upon submission — concerning the invalidity of which insofar as it might prejudice him, the Honourable Prusimski makes a formal protest); and they are acting and proceeding contrary to their own acknowledgement and recognition already confirmed by decrees, and thereby draw upon themselves the penalties prescribed by law, and will be obliged to be liable to them and likewise to return costs wrongfully caused to the complainant.

[The Boundary Line Continued — Mounds, Stawisko, and Cygany]

The Honourable Prusimski, having shown the corner mound and submitted proofs and lodged his protests, proceeding further with his boundary line and the boundary between Trąbczyn Mały or Nowa Wieś and Łukomia and running it toward the south — he went from the said corner mounds a little by a shortcut to the right, between south and west, and having crossed the road from Zagórów and Drzewce running towards Kalisz, Chocz, and Tomice as described above,

came a greater or lesser distance to an old wolf pit, well marked. Not far from this wolf pit he showed a sufficiently prominent mound having a roundness and a small ditch, that is a crown, around it — which he held to be a boundary mound between Łukomia and Trąbczyn — and beside this mound showed a track branching to the right from the Zagórów and Drzewce road, heading by a shortcut well to the right under the hill towards Łukomia, joining the Osiny road and running together with it further. Then, going on from the mound through pine forest towards the [sandy hill](https://www.google.com/maps/place/52%C2%B007'23.9%22N+17%C2%B052'55.4%22E) above the Osiny road — now the Pyzdry road — he came to the [cross road](https://www.google.com/maps/place/52%C2%B007'24.4%22N+17%C2%B052'53.5%22E) from Osiny running towards Łukomia, or rather to Łukom towards Pyzdry, crossing the Zagórów road here in this place. Having crossed it, he brought his court to the second mound, lying just beyond the Osiny road a few paces from the road, barely raised above the ground, but having a great likeness [to a mound] and a visible small ditch and roundness, upon which there stands a pine stump, already decayed at its edge, concealing the ditch in this place — which he holds to be a boundary mound of Łukomia and Trąbczyn.

From there, going further, he ran his boundary line south along that same Zagórów and Drzewce road to Kalisz, Chocz, and Tomice, passing over a small sandy hill described above, and so going two or three staj, with his own pine forest and Ostrowce woodland to the left, and [the land] now separated to the Ląd Convent through the compromise of 1761 running beside that same Zagórów or Kalisz road to the right — he came to a prominent mound, heaped by the compromise recently as a marker of the pine forest allotted to the Ląd Convent, on the right side of the Zagórów or Kalisz road, crushed by a fallen half-pine. From that mound, going on further along the same road through pine forest to the south and

showing his pine forest to the left and to the right towards Łukomia as before, going likewise two or more staj, he came to a dam, not very long, about one hundred łokci, not very high, and likewise to the [stawisko](https://www.google.com/maps/place/52%C2%B007'15.8%22N+17%C2%B053'01.8%22E) lying between hills and pine forest on the left side, empty, upon which is a sluice in the manner of a small weir. This stawisko or empty marsh, extending no further than one hundred łokci beyond the dam, and thereafter becoming wide, overgrown with clumps and various trees, having no mill race cut below the dam, now dry — and likewise the woodland of various kinds standing and lying around it and beside it, in part not inconsiderable, overturned and dried out.

And likewise the Ostrowce woodland, shown above at the corner mounds, touching on one side there the mounds and on this side here the said stawisko, lying beside the stawisko on this side to the left; and likewise a ditch from the said dam through the Zagórów or Kalisz road towards the Łukomia pond lying below [i.e., to the west] towards Łukomia, passing through from the stawisko both there and through marshland woodland; and likewise the Łukomia pond itself — called the new pond — lying on similar marshes, overgrown with clumps and various trees, having only a mill race cut below the dam and before the mill, sufficiently wide and long; and likewise the dam, well made, two staj long, wide, and high, and the mill on this pond — all of which the Honourable Prusimski, turning aside from his boundary line, showed to his court.

And thereupon at once he demonstrates to his court that by this pond and so great a dam, when the heir of Łukomia built it and the pond on such marshes — similar to the stawisko and lying to the right of the boundary line road belonging to it — they caused the flooding of the said stawisko belonging to Trąbczyn, as the Honourable Prusimski and his people allege, and likewise of the pastures, meadows, and woodlands upon that stawisko — further proving

the ownership of that empty stawisko, that is the empty marshes, belonging to Trąbczyn, he submits:

In the first place the partition among the late Honourable and Honest Wojciech, Mikołaj, and Stanisław Trąbczyński, brothers by birth, heirs of the whole Trąbczyn estate, attested in the Konin municipal court in 1522, on Thursday on the vigil of the Ascension of the Lord — by which partition Stanisław Trąbczyński receives at Nowa Wieś a pond with meadows;

In the second place he submits two decrees of 1589 in the Konin land court under one act, that is on Monday before Saints Vitus and Modestus — between the late Honourable Wojciech Trąbczyński, at that time heir only of a certain portion of the estates of Trąbczyn Wielki and Mały, Osiny, and the woodlands, as plaintiff on the one side, and the late Honourable Tomasz Łukomski and Barbara Brodzińska, widow of Sebastian Modliboski, heirs of the village of Łukomia, as defendants on the other side — concerning the flooding of the woodlands, pine forests, pastures, and meadows on the estate of Trąbczyn Wielki and Mały, Osiny, and the woodlands belonging to his portion, caused by a new pond built on the Łukomia estate, with a judicial site inspection assigned to the ground, ordered to be conducted by the defendant party and not the plaintiff — both issued and made;

And likewise he submits a third decree between the same parties in the Konin land court, issued after those decrees, by which Tomasz Łukomski was ordered to swear, with two noblemen, to the damages caused in the Trąbczyn pine forests, meadows, and pastures in the portion of the said Wojciech Trąbczyński by the flooding of the water.

And likewise he submits a fourth decree of deferral of that same oath for six years, between the same parties in the later year 1595, in the same Konin courts, on Monday after the Octave of Corpus Christi.

And finally he submits the partition among the Honourables and Honest Andrzej, Jan, and Władysław Trąbczyński, brothers by birth, heirs of the villages of Trąbczyn Mały and Wielki, Osiny, and the woodlands, of the sixth

October 1693, filed in the Pyzdry municipal court — by which partition the said Trąbczyński, dividing the said villages among themselves, apart from the ponds expressed in that partition, reserve to themselves the remaining ponds or rather empty marshes for their common use and fishing, and likewise retain for common pasture the flat marshes overgrown with clumps found in the pine forests, and moreover provide that none of them should take from the serfs what any serf may cut — all of this they stipulate.

And further, proving the ownership of the empty stawisko belonging to Trąbczyn and the encroachment thereon of the Honourable Chełmski, he submits several judicial site inspections and manifests lodged and initiated by the late Honourable Krzysztof Prusimski, heir of Trąbczyn with appurtenances, against the Honourable Chełmski, predecessors of the present party, concerning the appropriation of this stawisko and the Lusnia woodland, the violent felling of the meadows upon it, and the building of the dam on that stawisko — beginning from 1713 —

And likewise the Konin land court decree against the predecessors of the Honourable Chełmski on Monday after the Sunday Septuagesime in 1728, concerning that same stawisko and the transgressions committed upon it, with a field session assigned to the ground;

And likewise all other proceedings expressed in the schedule of the Honourable Starost, signed by his court, he submits — and likewise declares in court that sworn inquiries conducted pursuant to that decree upon the boundaries will be present and will be submitted in the further course of the cause.

And finally, that this stawisko has belonged to Trąbczyn from of old and Trąbczyn has used it — although at times the Honourables and Honest Chełmski encroached upon it — sworn inquiry, old and future, will attest. The Honourable Prusimski also says that since by the partition of 1522

at Nowa Wieś Stanisław Trąbczyński received a pond with meadows, and there is no other pond with or without meadows on the entire estate of Nowa Wieś except this present stawisko, he thereby proves by that partition that this stawisko has become depleted from that pond, and for that reason is called a stawisko or rather empty marshes in the later partition of 1693.

And therefore, from these proofs, holding the empty stawisko, that is the empty marshes, to be properly Trąbczyn's, and taking them into his boundary line, and protesting thereupon concerning the unjust and wrongful usurpation thereof, and concerning the dam and the flooding of meadows, pine forests, and [other land] — referring to the former manifests and judicial site inspections lodged against the heirs of Łukomia — the Honourable Prusimski went further from this place along that same beaten Zagórów or Kalisz road towards the west; and having crossed the ditch beside the very dam of that stawisko and coming out below the hill towards the old Kalisz or Zagórów road lying to the right, and showing at the same time a small field lying on the right side of the road belonging to Łukomia, he said that here beside this road there was a boundary mound but it has been ploughed up by the predecessor of the Honourable Chełmski — and in support submits the judicial site inspection and manifest made in 1726 at Pyzdry.

Going further from there along that same Zagórów road towards the west, leaving a small field to the right of that road belonging to Łukomia or Łomowo and the road peacefully used up to it, and on the other side to the left his own pine forest of pine on strips not very wide beyond the stawisko, which the people assert the former heirs of Trąbczyn forbade Łukomia to sow [into] and [which has] grown up — he came to the old third mound, not very raised above the ground, with a sufficiently good roundness, a sufficiently marked small ditch all around it, that is a crown, lying to the right of the Kalisz or Zagórów road beside a small oak on a slightly sandy spot — which

the heir of Trąbczyn held and said to be the true boundary mound of Łukomia or Łomowo with Trąbczyn. From that mound, going further along that same Zagórów road through pine forest in the same direction for several staj, showing blazes on certain pine trees and his pine forest to the left and [that] belonging to Łukomia or Łomowo to the right of the road, and saying that the pine forest has been used and enclosed by Trąbczyn up to that road — he came to the fourth mound, immediately more prominent than the preceding one, lying in a slightly low spot on the left side of the Zagórów or Kalisz road, having a roundness and a small ditch, that is a crown — holding it to be a boundary mound of the above-mentioned estates. And from that mound, taking the direction south along that same old road, having left the new [road] to the right, going through pine forest across a low area a greater or lesser distance of two staj, and having come out a little onto a low hill of sparse pine — he came to a road not very prominent running from Łomowo across to the olędry, and having crossed this road continued his boundary line along that same old Kalisz road southward, past and along the very headland of a small field a few strips wide and one staj long newly cleared in the pine by a Łomowo serf, as he alleges — he came to a second cross road running from Łomowo and Łukomia to the Trąbczyn olędry and joining the first [road] towards the olędry — and crossing this he came to the fifth mound, larger than the others, flat, having a small ditch round it and likewise a roundness, not very raised above the ground — holding it to be a boundary mound.

From there, going through very sparse pine forest not far, a greater or lesser distance of one and a half staj, to the right of the Łomowo field, along that same old Kalisz road leaving the new [road] to the right nearer the Łomowo field — going a greater or lesser distance of three staj and saying his pine forest is to the left and [that] belonging to Łukomia is to the right of the road and has been used as such formerly — he came to a drainage strip lying immediately to the left of the Zagórów or Kalisz road and touching the very end of the Kalisz road, and likewise to a prominent road running from Łomowo and

Łukomia by a shortcut across the Kalisz or Zagórów road past the very marsh across, and running towards Królikowo above that same marsh at first and then further away from the marsh through pine — and having crossed this road and gone a short distance he came to a second road also running from Łomowo and Łukomia along the Łomowo field itself towards the Kalisz ford, joining the Kalisz road at a diagonal about a staj above [i.e., to the north of] the ford — and joining the Kalisz road. And from there going along the Kalisz road nearer the Łomowo field, also newly cleared since the stakes upon it are standing, a greater or lesser distance of a staj, he came to the ford — not very great, now dry — called the Kalisz [ford], beside which there are also wooden footboards laid.

Having crossed this ford, he showed the road immediately branching to the right from the ford towards the west, leading to Grądzeń or the glassworks and also to Tomice past Grądzeń — branching higher up towards Grądzeń, as the people say — as expressed in the judicial site inspection, that is the field act conducted by the late Honourable Mieleński, Boundary Surveyor of Poznań, from a Tribunal decree of 1658. Having left that road to the right and turning left, southward, along the Kalisz road still at that same Kalisz ford, and having come out of the ford into pine forest, he showed, not far from that ford to the left of the Kalisz road, a blaze on a pine tree, sufficiently prominent and old — and thereupon submitting the above-mentioned judicial site inspection by the Poznań Boundary Surveyor Mieleński of 1658, proves thereby that the Honourable Trąbczyński, heir of Trąbczyn, having stood beside this Kalisz road, whether here or above before the ford, asserted and acknowledged it to be a boundary between Trąbczyn and Grądzeń, and likewise acknowledged the pine forest to the left of this road as Trąbczyn's and to the right as Grądzeń's. And likewise he proves that although the heir of Łukomia protested at that judicial site inspection against the heir of Trąbczyn — not, however, concerning the road, that it should not be a boundary, but only that he [Trąbczyński] attributed the boundary not to Łukomia but to Grądzeń.

And likewise the Ląd Convent, during that judicial site inspection and thereafter always held

that same road as a boundary, and established its Grądzeń boundary up to that road — although he now has it not with the Convent but with Łukomia and Łomowo. The Honourable Prusimski says this is because the Convent, having agreed to a compromise with the Honourable Chełmski after that judicial site inspection and after the three field decrees in 1761, voluntarily ceded that boundary and that ground from that road to the Honourable Chełmski, and allowed it to be marked above with mounds through that compromise. And that this is so he submits the same compromise decree between those same parties — made without his court appearance and that of other corner neighbours — in the same year 1761, filed in the Kalisz municipal court on Monday after the Sunday Cantate — which, having been made without his court appearance, cannot prejudice his former boundary and that old boundary road, nor can it abolish and alter the former Trąbczyn boundary. And he further says that although he now has no boundary with Grądzeń but with Łukomia, this makes no difference to Trąbczyn, whether to have a boundary with Grądzeń or with Łukomia — for Grądzeń is a portion of the Łukomia or Łomowo ground, and lies within and within the boundaries of Łukomia, sold off and separated to the Ląd Convent by Bochneta, heir of Łukomia and Łomowo.

And moreover, since the heir of Łukomia and Łomowo, protesting at that judicial site inspection against Trąbczyński — that he had attributed the boundary to Grądzeń and not to Łukomia — himself wished to have and acknowledged that Trąbczyn boundary with Łukomia and not with Grądzeń — and accordingly, pursuant to that same protest and acknowledgement of the heir of Łukomia, and the cession of ground by the Convent to Łukomia, and pursuant to the general boundaries of Łukomia or Łomowo determined by the Kalisz road — the Honourable Prusimski, wishing to have his boundary not with Grądzeń but with Łukomia, and not disputing with him the boundary or the ground ceded — is willing to concede it to him together with

the pine forest to the right of the Kalisz road belonging to Łukomia or Łomowo, and his own to the left belonging to Trąbczyn; and likewise acknowledging and asserting the Kalisz road as a boundary — insofar as it is confirmed by both corner and boundary mounds and blazes shown in the boundary line from the very corner mound all the way to this point, and by the long-standing use of the pine forest up to that road from of old by Trąbczyn — he went further from this place between Trąbczyn and Łukomia or Łomowo along that same Kalisz road southward about two staj, and brought his court to a ditch and a small bridge over the ditch, dug and placed, as he alleges, not very long ago by the Honourable Chełmski — whereupon the Honourable Prusimski protests here at once that this ditch, lying on the other side of the road to the left, was dug not very long ago in his absence on his own ground and in his own pine forest, and a small bridge placed upon it. Having crossed this bridge and going along that same road in one and the same direction southward and showing blazes on the pine trees, a greater or lesser distance of two staj, he came to the road from Zagórów and other villages running to Tomice, branching diagonally to the right from the beaten Kalisz road. And from there, going further along the Kalisz road southward, he came to a second cross beaten road from Trąbczyn, formerly through pine and now through the Trąbczyn olędry, to Tomice — running beside the Pyzdry corner mounds lying a half-mile from here — and joining the first Tomice road mentioned above a few staj from here below the hill, as the people maintain — beside which road he showed an old cross cut in a pine tree, standing to the left of the Kalisz road.

Having crossed this cross road of Trąbczyn or Tomice, the Honourable Prusimski ran his boundary line further between Trąbczyn and Łukomia or Łomowo through pine along that same Kalisz road, showing his pine forest to the left, long used and enclosed by Trąbczyn, and demonstrating blazes on pine trees; and leaving the pine forest lying to the right belonging to Łukomia or Łomowo — he came to a small drainage strip or small ford from the duck bogs, crossing through the Kalisz road to another drainage strip lying on the other side of the Kalisz road, and likewise to a road lying beside this drainage strip running from the Trąbczyn olędry

above the duck bogs [i.e., to the north of the duck bogs], joining the Kalisz road diagonally from the left — and also a road now newly made through the Kalisz road in that direction. Going also along these tracks, he showed blazes on the pine trees. Having crossed this drainage strip, ford, and road, he ran his boundary line further along the long drainage strip called the duck bogs lying close to the Kalisz road, along that same Kalisz road through pine southward, for several staj, and came to a small ravine cut by water from the duck bogs to other drainage strips lying on the other side of the Kalisz road. Having crossed this ravine, he showed an old pine tree with two blazes, forked, standing immediately beside this ravine and the Kalisz road to the left, on what appears to be a round and raised spot — which pine tree he held to be grown upon a [former] mound, and asserted that a boundary mound is actually here. From that ravine and the forked pine, going further along that same old Kalisz road to the left, leaving the newly made [road] to the right, and going several staj toward Studzienka below the hills there and towards those hills called — as they say — the tavern hills — when the Honourable Prusimski came straight opposite eight mounds which people call Cygany (Gypsies), lying one and a half staj from the boundary line and the Kalisz road — as to the first mound taken — towards the corduroy crossings and Czarny Bród to the east, above the Lusnia woodland [i.e., to the north of the Lusnia woodland], long and now so called and named so by the Honourable Chełmski himself, stretching there towards the corduroy crossings and Czarny Bród, and lying on this side of the Lusnia woodland with respect to the boundary line of the Honourable Prusimski —

the Honourable Prusimski, having come in this place opposite the above-mentioned mounds, standing five staj before Studzienka below the hills called — as they say — the tavern hills, and likewise before those hills — pausing a little to show further and determine his terminal point of his boundary with Łukomia or Łomowo, which he establishes at Studzienka and the tavern hills — still wishing to show here the further continuation of the boundary and his further boundary line, and the accuracy of what has already been run, and likewise the ownership of the grounds, pine forests, and woodlands — Ostrowce, Miąsykąt,

Dziadulina, and in particular the one called Lusnia — and likewise the empty stawisko, that is the empty marshes described above, taken into his boundary line and lying within his boundary line, and taken by the Honourable Chełmski in his boundary line — and likewise the unlawfulness of the judicial site inspection of 1592 as contrary to all documents expressed below, and in particular as contrary to the Lusnia woodland and the corner mound or convergence of Biskupice with Łukomia and Trąbczyn expressed in it — wishing to show, prove, and test all this to his court both by these mounds and blazes behind them, and above all by other documents and decrees regarding the eight newly and freshly heaped mounds and forty-one blazes made — he therefore requested his court for the review and description of those mounds and blazes. The court, wishing to inform itself in this cause as thoroughly and precisely as possible, permitted this — and accordingly the Honourable Prusimski, having left the said Kalisz road and his boundary line, brought his court one and a half staj from the Kalisz road to the first of these eight mounds, lying in low ground beside a small drainage strip, sufficiently prominent and evident, having a roundness and a crown, overgrown with heather, lying at a distance in this place from the Lusnia woodland on this side of the boundary line of the Honourable Prusimski in the pine forest to the east towards the corduroy crossings and Czarny Bród. And from that mound, going further also to the east towards the corduroy crossings and Czarny Bród a considerable distance, he showed his court seven more such prominent and visible mounds in the pine forest above the said Lusnia woodland [i.e., to the north of it] — at first one and a half staj further, then at a staj from that same Lusnia woodland — lying one after another at various intervals of three, four, five staj, stretching toward the corduroy crossings for several staj, occupying and taking in the whole Lusnia woodland. And likewise he showed the last mound of these eight called Cygany — that is the eighth — lying one staj wider from the place where the Honourable Chełmski marked out and formed his corner mound at the very Lusnia woodland itself, and further and above that place and corner mound towards the east towards the corduroy crossings and Czarny Bród, reaching beyond and enclosing that place and corner mound. And likewise, after the conclusion of those mounds,

leading his court further to the east towards the corduroy crossings and Czarny Bród for several staj, he showed his court forty-one blazes on pine trees — which mounds and blazes, that they are on proper Trąbczyn ground, freshly and newly heaped and made from the corduroy crossings above the Lusnia woodland [i.e., at the northern end of the Lusnia woodland] at the great expense and harm of the Trąbczyn ground extending still considerably further — he proves by the following documents, namely:

First, a manifest together with a judicial site inspection by the late Honourable Anna née Słończyc Trąbczyńska, widow of the late Stanisław Trąbczyński, and likewise of the Honourable Maciej Dybryński, possessors of the villages and estates of Trąbczyn, against the late Honourables Zofia née Złotka Kożuchowska, mother, and her sons Andrzej and Jan Kożuchowski, heirs of Biskupice and others — concerning the armed incursion onto Trąbczyn ground and the transgressions attempted there, and concerning forty-one blazes made on pine trees beginning from the corduroy crossings above the Lusnia woodland [i.e., at its northern end], freshly made, and eight mounds newly and freshly heaped on the ground — lodged in the Pyzdry municipal court on Saturday before Holy Trinity in 1644; and thereafter a default decree (default) in the same matter at the suit of the said Honourable Anna née Słończyc, remaining widow, and of Marcin, Wojciech, Władysław, and Andrzej Trąbczyński, and likewise Maciej Dybryński, heirs and possessors of the villages of Trąbczyn with appurtenances — against the said Honourables Kożuchowska, mother and sons, and Łukasz Biskupski, heirs of the villages of Biskupice and Bystrzyce — in the Konin land court on Monday after the Octave of Corpus Christi of the same year.

And likewise he submits a second decree in the same Konin land court in the same matter between these parties, in the same year on Monday after Saint Hedwig, with a motion to the Tribunal admitted by the plaintiff party, that is the Trąbczyńskis, after a delay to the documents granted to the defendant party as unnecessary in an action of fact.

He further submits a Piotrków Tribunal decree in the same matter between these parties, on Wednesday on the very feast of Saint Prisca, Virgin and Martyr, of 1645 — by which a judicial site inspection and sworn inquiry upon the ground of the alleged injury is assigned, to be conducted by the Kalisz Boundary Surveyor within six weeks, with the appointed date in the Tribunal reserved for the further adjudication of the cause. And likewise he submits the recognition of Jan Masłek of Osiny, Court Summoner, and of the Honourable Andrzej Wejski, nobleman, at the instance of the Honourables Trąbczyński, against the Honourables Kożuchowski and Biskupski, heirs of Biskupice and Bystrzyce, in the Pyzdry municipal court on Thursday before the Sunday called Invocavit of 1645 — in which they state that the Honourable Trąbczyński was prepared pursuant to the Tribunal decree to conduct the judicial site inspection concerning the making of forty-one blazes and the heaping of eight mounds, and that they attempted and were on the ground — but since neither the other party nor the Boundary Surveyor had arrived, they declared this in the municipal court. And likewise he submits a second Piotrków Tribunal decree between those same parties in the same matter, in the same year 1645 on the Monday called Reminiscere — by which a second judicial site inspection and sworn inquiry on the ground of the alleged injury is ordered, with the restoration of the appointed date in the Tribunal. Then further he submits a third Piotrków Tribunal decree between those same parties in the same matter, from sworn inquiries and the judicial site inspection conducted by the Boundary Surveyor, of 1646, on Thursday after the Feast of the Immaculate Conception of the Blessed Virgin Mary — by which Łukasz Biskupski, having incurred the penalties, was ordered to swear in the Konin land court with two noblemen to the said forty-one blazes made on pine trees and eight mounds newly and freshly heaped — while the Kożuchowskis were released.

[Translator's note: Quadendo paenas succubitas: asterisked in the source as uncertain. The phrase appears to describe the penalty incurred; succubitas does not correspond to standard Latin but may be a distorted form of succubiturus (having incurred / about to undergo). Translated according to the most plausible sense.]

And likewise he submits the Konin land court decree between the same Honourables and Honest Trąbczyński and Łukasz Biskupski, from that Tribunal decree,

of 1647, on Monday before Saint Hedwig — by which, pursuant to the formula in the Tribunal decree, he was ordered to swear; and having performed it and sworn off the said marks and mounds — as not made and heaped by him — although alone without two noblemen — was from any further proceedings acquitted.

Having thus submitted these documents and actually shown to his court the mounds described above and the blazes above the said Lusnia woodland, and having shown and remonstrated to his court the position and actual existence of them — the Honourable Prusimski says and proves in his court by those same mounds, blazes, and documents: that since these mounds and blazes actually, according to these documents, are above the Lusnia woodland — so and not otherwise called by the people and by the Honourable Chełmski himself — and moreover lie still remote, at a staj from the Lusnia woodland, and the whole of that Lusnia woodland, and likewise Dziadulina, Miąsykąt, the stawisko or empty marshes, and the Ostrowce woodland described above, lie in the middle of his boundary line or within his boundary line, taken into his boundary line — and finally the whole boundary line and corner mounds of the Honourable Chełmski reach and enclose them — and moreover the last Cygany mound, that is the eighth, lying further about a staj to the east towards the corduroy crossings and Czarny Bród, reaches above and beyond that place and the corner mound established and formed by the Honourable Chełmski at the very Lusnia woodland, and that mound together with its other companions and the blazes stretching further toward the corduroy crossings beyond the last mound above the Lusnia woodland, as shown in the remonstration — and likewise documents attest the further extent of the Lusnia woodland and other Trąbczyn ground in length, and also in breadth on the other side, further from the mounds towards the south —

and these mounds, blazes, and the documents submitted above set forth that they were newly and freshly heaped and made, beginning from the corduroy crossings above the Lusnia woodland, on proper Trąbczyn ground, to the considerable detriment and harm of Trąbczyn ground still extending considerably further, and assert it — and these are actually found above the Lusnia woodland. Therefore the Honourable Prusimski says that since these mounds and blazes

are actually above the Lusnia woodland and still lie remote from that woodland, those same mounds and blazes — apart from all other proofs — demonstrate not only the ground on which they themselves lie, but also the ground between them and the Lusnia woodland, and finally the entire Lusnia woodland, Dziadulina, Miąsykąt, Ostrowce, and the stawisko or empty marshes — in short, the entire and complete ground and the olędry unjustly taken and claimed by the boundary line of the Honourable Chełmski and lying within his boundary line — as enclosing and taking in all this, openly vindicate and prove it for Trąbczyn — and the actual substance and ownership of all these, and as they must necessarily and are Trąbczyn's — sufficiently prove and set forth. And finally not only these themselves prove the ownership and substance of the grounds and woodlands expressed above, but also demonstrate more broadly still, in breadth and in length, the extent of his Trąbczyn grounds and pine forests, and likewise the further continuation of the boundary line and boundary with Łukomia or Łomowo — halted opposite these mounds on the Kalisz road — and which must extend all the way to Studzienka and the hills and tavern bogs to the corner mound there established. And moreover the Honourable Prusimski says that these mounds and blazes not only assert and confirm everything expressed above, but finally approve and corroborate the whole boundary line run to the place described above in its entirety, and likewise everything within the boundary line his — and assert and acknowledge his whole boundary line as accurate and good, confirmed by documents both written and on the ground by mounds and blazes, and likewise by long-standing use — while on the contrary, taking those same mounds and blazes, they overturn and rebuke the entire and general boundary line of the Honourable Chełmski as contrary to those mounds and blazes, and as formed and marked through the proper grounds, pine forests, and woodlands and the stawisko, that is the empty marshes of Trąbczyn — and likewise his corner mounds established and formed on Trąbczyn ground, and the grounds wrongly appropriated by him to himself and his reaching to Biskupice, and likewise the judicial site inspection of 1592 as contrary in all respects to these mounds, proofs, and documents submitted above and below.

And finally, that the Honourable Chełmski through

the Trąbczyn boundary, confirmed by so many proofs, according to the above-mentioned judicial site inspection of 1592, can neither reach the boundary nor the corner mound of Biskupice — the aforesaid proofs and the Trąbczyn ground and boundary standing in the way — and ought not to — and cannot have a corner mound with Biskupice — all this is expressed and set forth not only by those mounds and blazes, but also by the Łukomia heirs' own judicial site inspection of 1593, made in the Pyzdry municipal court on Thursday on the very feast of Saint Mary Magdalene — one year after the above-mentioned judicial site inspection — which sets forth that the heirs, conducting it, whether going themselves or wishing to lead [a party] to the Biskupice boundary or corner mound, could reach Biskupice by no other means than by the boundaries of Osiny — these are the words of that same judicial site inspection — and the boundaries of Osiny are the same as those of Trąbczyn.

And to this, further proving in that place the unlawfulness and great doubtfulness and the contrariness of the judicial site inspection of 1589, conducted in the field and attested in 1592, to the documents as well as to the mounds expressed above — the Honourable Prusimski says and brings before his court: that this judicial site inspection was conducted not by the heir of all of Trąbczyn but only by Wojciech Trąbczyński, who had only a portion thereof, past the other co-heirs, his four nephews, then with him co-heirs of Trąbczyn — and moreover that it was conducted by the guardian of those nephews to their detriment — and proving likewise the following circumstances, the Honourable Prusimski says again and brings before his court, along with the documents expressed below: that since this judicial site inspection of 1592 was ordered to be conducted not by the plaintiff, that is Wojciech Trąbczyński, but by the defendants, that is the heirs of Łukomia — by four Kalisz land decrees — and it was conducted and attested at the instance of Wojciech Trąbczyński as plaintiff, it may be that the heir of Łukomia conducted it at his instance, without the presence and knowledge

of the same, and had it attested to the detriment of him and of the Trąbczyn grounds. Secondly, that this judicial site inspection has something in it — namely, that it was conducted in the field in 1589 around the Feast of Saint James, and only three years later, in 1592 in the Konin municipal court around the Feast of the Immaculate Conception of the Blessed Virgin Mary, was attested. Even if it were granted from the outset, even if it had been conducted by Wojciech Trąbczyński himself and attested at his instance — it is not fitting: for Wojciech Trąbczyński at that time was not alone heir of all of Trąbczyn, but together with his four nephews, heir of Trąbczyn, that is the whole Trąbczyński estate — as the judicial site inspection itself shows, since it is attested only for his portion. And moreover, when this judicial site inspection was conducted in 1589, that Wojciech Trąbczyński was at that time guardian of his four nephews; then, having ceased to be guardian of those his nephews, he at once began a lawsuit with them in 1591 — and so while that lawsuit was ongoing between them, only then the attestation of this judicial site inspection in 1592 in the Konin municipal court to the detriment of his nephews and his own wards — and possibly by prearrangement and previously arranged [arrangement] with the heirs of Łukomia as well — took place. For the said Wojciech Trąbczyński, having a lawsuit with them concerning the flooding of the Trąbczyn and Osiny grounds, pine forests, and woodlands, having obtained a decree against the heirs of Łukomia ordering an oath or rather a swearing-off of the damages — as attested by the Konin land court decree of 1595 — limited that same oath for six years.

And therefore, since this judicial site inspection was made and conducted in such a manner — by an heir of only a portion, Wojciech Trąbczyński — and it is not clear whether even by himself — contrary to law and all proofs both written and shown on the ground, contrary to the position of the villages and places, past the common heirs and his nephews and wards, to their detriment, and likewise to the detriment of the old boundaries — and one might say privately and without any decree since it was not ordered for the plaintiff — and moreover since not immediately upon its very conduct

but only three years after its conduct in the field, in the Konin municipal court — and at that during a lawsuit and quarrel with his nephews — it was attested — it is therefore shown to be not only subject to great doubt, but also contrary to all proofs and to the law itself, and by reason of the statutory time-limit provisions of the law governing it, of no worth, and indeed reprehensible and abolished by the law itself. And moreover, that this judicial site inspection is ordered to be observed only insofar as it has not been and does not prove contrary to other proofs — by the last decree of the Bydgoszcz Tribunal of 1768.

And so neither the boundary line of the Honourable Chełmski and the claims to the grounds taken or marked by his boundary line — and in particular as to the corner mound and convergence of Biskupice, to which he can only reach by necessarily passing through the Trąbczyn grounds and boundary, as he now does — can find support in this judicial site inspection, nor ought it to; nor can the Honourable Chełmski establish for himself boundaries and corner mounds within the Trąbczyn grounds and boundary on the foundation of it — of the nullity of which and in respect of which the Honourable Prusimski, having protested in his court from the reason and knowledge of those prejudices now formed from the documents and position of places — submits in proof and confirmation of all that is expressed above the following documents:

And in the first place four Kalisz land decrees in the Konin land court on Monday before Saint Vitus of 1589, between Wojciech Trąbczyński as plaintiff on the one side and the heirs of Łukomia as defendants on the other, concerning the flooding of the grounds, meadows, pine forests, and woodlands on the Trąbczyn and Osiny estates caused by a newly built pond, dam, and the holding back of water on the Łukomia estate — by which he demonstrates to his court that the judicial site inspection assigned by those decrees was ordered not for the plaintiff but for the defendant party.

And likewise, secondly, he submits a decree of acquittal concerning the digging of a ditch and the ploughing up of a beaten road — between the same Honourable Wojciech, uncle and guardian, and Wawrzyniec, Rachwall, Walenty, and Stanisław, his nephews Trąbczyński, on the one side, and the late Honourable Mikołaj Trąbczyński, heir of a portion of Szetlew, on the other — in the same Konin land court, in the same year and under the same act, made three years before the judicial site inspection — by which he proves that Wojciech Trąbczyński was heir not of all of Trąbczyn but was guardian of his above-mentioned nephews, co-heirs of Trąbczyn with him.

And likewise he submits the above-mentioned judicial site inspection of that same year 1589, on Monday on the vigil of Saint James the Apostle, conducted in the field pursuant to the above-mentioned four decrees from the portion of the Honourable Trąbczyński himself, and attested in the Konin municipal court on Thursday after the Feast of the Immaculate Conception of the Blessed Virgin Mary of 1592 — by which he likewise demonstrates to his court that Wojciech Trąbczyński was not heir of all of Trąbczyn, and that this judicial site inspection was made during guardianship and attested during the lawsuit and quarrel over the partition occurring with him in the years expressed above.

And likewise he submits the partition among the said Wawrzyniec, Rachwall, Walenty, and Stanisław Trąbczyński, nephews, by then released from guardianship, on the one side, and the Honourable late Wojciech Trąbczyński, their uncle, on the other — concerning the division of Trąbczyn with appurtenances — of 1591, on Monday after the Sunday Jubilate — made nearly two years after the judicial site inspection was conducted in the field and three quarters of a year before its attestation — attested in the Konin municipal court by the Burgrave — by which he likewise proves that Wojciech Trąbczyński was not heir of all of Trąbczyn;

Both after that judicial site inspection conducted in the field and before its attestation, [as] he was in a lawsuit with his nephews. And likewise, proving that even after this judicial site inspection there was no single ownership in Trąbczyn with appurtenances, he submits the decree of 1595 in the Konin Kalisz land court on Monday after Saint Hedwig — between the same Wojciech Trąbczyński and the Ląd Convent, concerning the flooding by the Tarszew mill

at Kopanina of the Trąbczyn meadows belonging to his portion, with the damage [podatek od] relating to his portion alone, apart from the other co-heirs with him.

And likewise he submits the agreement between the late Honourable and Honest Szczęsna née Popów, widow of the late Honourable Jan Trąbczyński, as mother, on the one side, and the Honourables Rachwall, Walenty, and Stanisław, her sons Trąbczyński, on the other — concerning the allotment of a portion in the Trąbczyński estate — made upon ratification in the Konin municipal court on Monday after the Exaltation of the Holy Cross of 1596, and filed.

And likewise he submits the partition concerning the division of the same Trąbczyński estate between the Honourables late Andrzej, Jan, and Władysław Trąbczyński, brothers by birth, of 1693 — already placed above, extracted from the Pyzdry municipal court records.

And finally the Honourable Prusimski says that the predecessors of the Honourable Chełmski never used this judicial site inspection at the past sessions and judicial site inspections, and Biskupice never asserted any claim to the Lusnia woodland nor established a corner mound with Łukomia — for Biskupice has no convergence and no claim to the Lusnia woodland, nor any corner mound with Łukomia, either here or in any other place given that wall Trąbczyna — but they have their corner mound with Łazy and Trąbczyn Wielki, near Czarny Bród and the mound called Tomal, lying above [i.e., to the north of] the Siedleska or Jarocin road.

And therefore the Honourable Chełmski wrongly and unjustly establishes and forms for himself a corner mound as though of Łukomia, Trąbczyn, and Biskupice at the Lusnia woodland, and wrongly takes three mounds or stumps from among six lying together in that place — having no likeness whatsoever to mounds, not even round but rather oblong — as his corner mounds; and wrongly brings and draws the heir of Biskupice — thinking as one with him and taking a lead against Trąbczyn — to acknowledge those mounds or corner mounds established by him, and likewise to

claims to proper Trąbczyn grounds, and thereby to dispute. Concerning all this, and concerning the wrongly formed corner mound from three stumps — which three others similar are beside them — and concerning the whole boundary line formed and marked through the proper grounds and woodlands of Trąbczyn belonging and used from of old by Trąbczyn, and for several decades cleared and occupied without any objection by the olędry in the time of his predecessors, and finally concerning the unjust taking and seizure by that same boundary line of the Lusnia, Dziadulina, Miąsykąt, and Ostrowce woodlands, and likewise the empty stawisko or empty marshes, and likewise twelve olędry with inn, fields, and meadows — in short, against the entire boundary line of the Honourable Chełmski as unjust and wrongly and unlawfully formed and marked against law and justice — and likewise for its nullity; and thereupon also at the same time against the Honourable Gałecki, Starost of Bydgoszcz, heir of Biskupice and the Bystrzyce deserted lands: that he, notwithstanding the above-mentioned reasons and documents and the swearing-off of the mounds and blazes by the former heir of Biskupice, having no claim to the Lusnia woodland and no convergence with Łukomia and being unable to have one — proceeding wrongly and unjustly to the detriment and harm of Trąbczyn ground in league with the Honourable Chełmski — wrongly and without any documents, from memory alone, acknowledges in particular the corner mound established by the Honourable Chełmski wrongly and contrary to the above-mentioned documents and mounds at the very Lusnia woodland; and thereby intends to appropriate and [to take] from Trąbczyn the rest of the Lusnia woodland still left by the Honourable Chełmski, and likewise the ground between the Lusnia woodland and the mounds and blazes — and on the other side acknowledges to the Honourable Chełmski the ground and pine forest running from here toward the Kalisz road above the Lusnia woodland [i.e., to the north of the Lusnia woodland] together with the Cygany mounds — and thus both of them divide Trąbczyn's proper ground between themselves and content each other at Trąbczyn's expense. After this inspection and description of the eight mounds and blazes

described above, and the remonstration made to his court and the proofs and protests submitted above — the Honourable Prusimski, having returned to his boundary line and the Kalisz road and having come to the place where his boundary line was halted — says and proves to his court by the above-mentioned documents: that since in those documents it is expressed that the said mounds and blazes were heaped and made with great detriment and harm to Trąbczyn ground — and these are actually shown at the present appointed date — it follows without any question that the Trąbczyn ground must extend further in breadth to the south and in length to the east.

And it therefore follows that the further boundary line of Trąbczyn with Łukomia or Łomowo must still continue, and must have its determination of the terminal point at the old corner mounds or convergence of three estates at the tavern hills and bogs and Studzienka established from of old — as expressed in Mieleński's judicial site inspection — and accordingly the Honourable Prusimski from this place continued his further boundary line, or rather boundary with Łukomia or Łomowo, southward along that same old Kalisz road, leaving the new [road] to the right, about five staj through pure somewhat sparse pine, and showing blazes on the pine trees and likewise the Rozciszew folwark of the Honourable Chełmski, his inherited property lying in the pine on the right side of the boundary line and Kalisz road a greater or lesser distance of six staj from here, visible from here — came to Studzienka, not very deep or wide, faced with no stonework, which is said to have been formerly faced with brick but demolished by the heirs of Łukomia — and likewise to the long hills lying respectively cross to the boundary line through the Kalisz road at one end between east and south towards the Jarocin road running from Szetlew and likewise towards Gąsiennice for

[Translator's note: Nieocembrowana: having no cembrowanie, that is no lining or stonework. The word refers to the stone or timber lining of a well, spring, or cistern. Translated as "faced with no stonework".]

several staj, and at the other end towards the Rozciszew folwark to the west for about three staj — lying immediately beside that Studzienka. And likewise the drainage strips on the other side of the hills on both sides of the Kalisz road, close to those hills, through which the Kalisz road passes and beyond the hills runs between the drainage strips — which hills and bogs the heir and people of Trąbczyn say, pursuant to Mieleński's judicial site inspection of 1658 by the Poznań Boundary Surveyor, are called the tavern hills and bogs — and that they hold this name from two inns or taverns, the ruins of which are still extant and were shown to his court: one, the Królikowo [tavern], above the Kalisz road; and the other, the Oborsk [tavern], above the Królikowo road to Toporów, Tomice, and further, lying in the pine about a half-mile from here. And likewise in that same place immediately beyond the hills on the side where the bogs lie, the Honourable Prusimski showed his court a large stone, three łokci long, not very raised above the ground, lying beside the Kalisz road — holding it to be a guardian of the corner mound. And likewise he showed the road there from that stone from the Kalisz road branching to the left westward towards Rozciszew, running beside the drainage strip alongside the above-described hills to the left and on to Rozciszew — prominent and old. And likewise he showed, straight opposite Studzienka beside the very Kalisz road, a large old pine tree with an old burnt cross cut into it standing there — and on the other side of Studzienka he showed a boggy marsh lying about half a staj from Studzienka below the hills to the left, about three staj long, not very wide, overgrown with various trees and clumps.

Having thus shown these marks to his court and establishing here his terminal point or corner mound, he says that here is the confluence of three estates, namely

his Trąbczyn to the left and Łukomia to the right of the road — and that both these estates have used pine forest up to this point — and of a third estate, having no definitive document at present as to which third estate should reach here — especially since no corner neighbours from Tomice or Biskupice appeared, and they are to be treated in default — he held to be Biskupice, and placed here the corner mound of these three estates, namely Trąbczyn, Łukomia or Łomowo, and Biskupice, and determined his boundary with Łukomia or Łomowo with a reservation of his legal rights regarding the other estates.

After this boundary line run and determined at the terminal point at Studzienka and the above-described tavern hills — as to the Trąbczyn boundary with Łukomia or Łomowo — although the Honourable Chełmski does not appear in refutation of the boundary line of the Honourable Prusimski and to show his own, and is to be treated in default — together with the corner neighbours — the court, wishing to inform itself as thoroughly as possible in all respects in the judging and establishing of this cause and the boundary lines of both parties — especially since the Honourable Prusimski requests this and asks his court for the execution and inspection of the boundary line and its description — the court therefore offers to proceed to the review of the boundary line of the Honourable Chełmski, insofar as it has been marked out. And meanwhile, having suspended for a time the review of the boundary line of the Honourable Chełmski — since the matter is proceeding in a split court — and since the Honourable Prusimski, having been issued by the court a judicial declaration for the bringing of a sworn surveyor to produce a map, brought in the course of his boundary line a sworn surveyor, namely the Reverend Father Benedykt Woronowski, Doctor of Theology and Philosophy and actual Professor of Philosophy at the Poznań Academy —

the court, having informed itself from his instrument and oath that he is sworn, orders that the said Most Reverend Father Woronowski, surveyor, delineate as precisely as possible the boundary line of both parties, already run in one Trąbczyn boundary, and likewise the inspection of the mounds and blazes, and the position of all adjacent places and other places even outside the boundary lines — shown at various sessions before and needed for the remonstration the present boundary lines —

during the course of the present commission, and form a map — and to present it to his court for its verification and signing, at the determination of that commission, to the Most Honourable citizen President. And this immediately without delay. After which sentence promulgated by his court, the present commission court, resolving the above-mentioned suspension, proceeded and went to the review and description of the boundary line of the Honourable Chełmski, insofar as it had been marked out with stakes.

[Section 5: Review of Chełmski's Boundary Line — Trąbczyn–Biskupice Boundary — Sworn Inquiry — Resolution on the Starting Point]

And so the Honourable Prusimski, having taken his stand at the corner mound in the place where the Honourable Chełmski establishes his corner mound — that is, at the head, or beginning, of the woodland called Ostrowce, at the Osiny road itself, which runs between the two mounds, supposedly corner mounds of the Honourable Chełmski but in fact Drzewce and Trąbczyn wall mounds — and having led his court out onto that road toward Osiny, showed his court that this road comes not from Trąbczyn but from Osiny, and falls into the Zagórów road a few staj from here, and then also crosses the Zagórów road below the hill and goes by way of Łukom to Pyzdry — as the past decrees, one of 1759 and the other of 1760, submitted and described in the boundary line, also express.

And that this road between Trąbczyn, Drzewce, Łukomia, Wrąbczyn, and Zagórów, as the judicial site inspection states, can by no means go and does not go in any way other than as immediately described to his court — this he submitted and in that name protested. And likewise he showed his court the Ostrowce woodland — his own, already shown in his boundary line at his corner mound and explained by the documents there submitted — belonging to Trąbczyn from of old. And likewise beside that same road a few paces he showed a prominent mound — the same as the others — one being #14 of the Drzewce and Trąbczyn wall mounds, and another, that is #15, likewise a Drzewce wall mound with Trąbczyn — lying with a road between them a half staj apart — which the Honourable Chełmski takes as his corner mounds. And likewise he showed a very small hillock

on which a pine stands, or rather a rise of ground swollen up with a pine, lying about four paces from the first mound — which the Honourable Chełmski at past sessions, as the field decrees attest, always took and held to be the third corner mound — but now, having abandoned that hillock, takes in its place an obvious stump as the third corner mound, lying nearly one staj from those two, above the Ostrowce woodland toward the stawisko — which the court, seeing it shown, considers from what was observed on the ground to be a stump. And likewise in refutation of — that these two mounds, #14 and #15, lying eighty łokci apart, taken as corner mounds by the Honourable Chełmski, are not corner mounds but are actually wall mounds of Drzewce with Trąbczyn — and that the two mounds below #14, and further the others, lie at the same distance from one another as those two, while the others lie variously and unevenly as described below — he therefore for this purpose showed his court, going from here toward his own corner mound westward, along the former boundary of the Honourable Chełmski formed supposedly between Drzewce and Łukomia at two sessions in 1759 and 1760 and now abandoned: mounds of the same form and appearance as those two —

the first from #14 at eighty łokci;

the second after it at seventy łokci;

the third after that at one hundred łokci;

the fourth at one hundred and twenty łokci;

the fifth at eighty łokci;

the sixth likewise at eighty łokci;

the seventh at one hundred łokci;

the eighth at one hundred and forty łokci;

the ninth at two hundred łokci;

the tenth at one hundred and forty łokci;

the eleventh at two hundred łokci;

the twelfth, thirteenth, and fourteenth at one hundred and forty łokci each —

as shown by measurement with an eight-łokieć measuring staff — lying there and held and taken by the Honourable Chełmski at past sessions as wall mounds supposedly in a wall between Drzewce and Łukomia, reaching to his corner mound.

From that corner mound, going further south above the Ostrowce woodland — which the Honourable Prusimski says has been so called from of old and now, and submits the judicial site inspection of 1644 upon it and the proceeding expressed above — for several staj, he showed his court two hillocks, bulbous and elongated, more resembling fallen trees than mounds, and having no resemblance to mounds whatsoever, neither roundness nor ditches — one on which a birch stands, and the other beside a pine running toward the stawisko — marked with stakes. And going further from there he came to the stawisko itself or empty marshes, where likewise above that stawisko he showed a third hillock of the same kind as the two described above. From there going further south through the empty stawisko or marshes for about three staj:

he showed his court shrubs, large clumps, and a great number of various trees both standing and fallen, and the meadows and pastures upon it. And likewise he showed his court that those marshes or stawisko are uniform throughout and stretch toward Osiny past Łazy, through the olędry to Czarny Bród — and there is no watershed in this place where the other party's boundary line runs, nor is any apparent — and that this stawisko lies within the position of Trąbczyn Mały and Osiny, behind the Ostrowce woodland, and has belonged to Trąbczyn from of old — in proof of their ownership submitting the two Trąbczyn partitions, one of 1522 in the Konin [court] and a second of 1693 in the Pyzdry municipal court, and likewise the numerous judicial site inspections and protests of the former heirs of Trąbczyn expressed in his boundary line. He likewise shows the boundary line through that stawisko — quite wide, cut with no small damage to the trees themselves.

And likewise going further and leaving the stawisko, he showed the meadows above the stawisko, and the boundary line of the Honourable Chełmski through those meadows, not far from an olędry cottage standing to the left of the boundary line toward Osiny. Going further from there and coming out onto a burnt [zgorzyłe] area between the marshes, he showed a small field worked and held by the Trąbczyn olędry for more than thirty years.

From that small field going south for about three staj through the large marshes, overgrown from of old with clumps and shrubs and belonging to Nowa Wieś and Osiny, he brought his court to the olędry fields — which fields the olędry have peacefully cleared and worked for themselves over a period of about thirty years in the time of the predecessors of the Honourable Chełmski, and those predecessors of the Honourable Chełmski said nothing to them about it and did not encroach on that ground. Going further from there through the olędry field, he brought his court to the road from Trąbczyn to Tomice — formerly through pine and now through the olędry. Having crossed this road, and going through a low or marshy area through the olędry fields, directing himself between east and south, to the left from south, he showed his court an elongated hillock having no resemblance to a mound, neither roundness nor ditch, marked as a mound, lying between the olędry cottages — to the right that of the Diligent Kinos and to the left that of the Diligent Meller. And going further from there through the olędry field between east and south, he came to a sandy hill on which a Lutheran school stands. Leaving that school on the hill to the left and taking part of the cemetery beside it on the right, and going through that cemetery, he showed his court the boundary line of the Honourable Chełmski along that same hill bending very sharply between east and south; and on that hill showed a spot with not the slightest resemblance to a mound, marked as a mound. Going from there still more between east and south for several staj along that same not-very-large and ploughed-up hill, he came to the cottage of the Diligent Betka, the settler. Having taken that cottage into his boundary line to the right, he turned along that same hill straight west. Going from there about two staj along that same hill and having come to some young pines [chojary] on that hill, he turned left by a shortcut through fields and strips again between south and west — and going a greater or lesser distance of four staj, he came through meadows to the marshy Lusnia woodland — one staj wide — and having reached the other side of the Lusnia woodland, at that same Lusnia woodland, twenty paces from the woodland and one staj from the Cygany mounds:

he showed his court the corner mound formed by the Honourable Chełmski, and six hillocks or stumps or corner mounds lying together in a heap — elongated, having no roundness and not even any ditches around them, and not the slightest resemblance to mounds — of which three are marked as corner mounds. He reports that the Honourable Chełmski, heir of Łukomia and Łomowo, and likewise Gałecki, Starost of Bydgoszcz, heir of Biskupice, acting as one to the detriment of Trąbczyn, taking those three hillocks or stumps as corner mounds, establish a corner mound here in this place and acknowledge it to each other — which they do as unjustly and wrongly as it is contrary to all proofs. The Honourable Prusimski likewise shows his court in refutation of those their supposed hillocks and corner mound three other hillocks or stumps. And moreover, reminding his court of the documents submitted above at the remonstration of the Cygany mounds and blazes, he says: that if the documents assert and show the eight Cygany mounds and the blazes to have been newly and freshly made on Trąbczyn ground above the Lusnia, and these are actually here above the Lusnia — and moreover since the heir of Biskupice, through swearing off those mounds and blazes, acknowledged the ground to be Trąbczyn's and made no claim to that ground at that time and did not defend himself to the effect that it was Biskupice ground, and Trąbczyn used that ground together with the mounds and the Lusnia woodland —

therefore as the ground on which the mounds and blazes are, and which lies between the mounds and the Lusnia, and likewise the entire Lusnia woodland, are manifestly shown to be Trąbczyn's — so no Biskupice ground can reach here, nor can there be any Biskupice corner mound or convergence. And it therefore follows that the judicial site inspection of 1589 — conducted in the field and attested in the Konin [municipal court] in 1592 — upon which the Honourable Chełmski bases his entire boundary line and corner mounds, is contrary not only to written documents

but also to those on the ground, and to the position of places. The corner mounds — both this one and the first at the head of the Ostrowce woodland — and the whole general boundary line of the Honourable Chełmski are wrong and unjust. And likewise the claim to the empty Trąbczyn stawisko, under the pretext of the pond which the Honourable Chełmski still has on Łukomia — called the new pond — is unjust and unwarranted.

And so the stawisko and the other woodlands — Ostrowce, Miąsykąt, Dziadulina — and likewise the olędry, meadows, and fields have been wrongly taken from Trąbczyn by the boundary line and corner mounds of the Honourable Chełmski, contrary to all counter-proofs and proofs formed and run; and likewise the corner mounds — the first at the Ostrowce woodland and the Osiny road, acknowledged to him by the Convent, and the second at the Lusnia woodland by the heir of Biskupice, by prearranged arrangement — are acknowledged unjustly and dishonestly. Concerning all this and in respect of all this — and concerning the unjust and unlawful acknowledgement of corner mounds to him, both at the starting point and at the terminal point — and likewise concerning the invalidity of the corner mounds and their acknowledgement, and of the entire boundary line of the Honourable Chełmski, formed and marked out through Trąbczyn's own ground, and of the judicial site inspection of 1592 as attested — as contrary to all proofs, law, and justice, and to the terminality of the law — against the Honourable Chełmski, Gałecki, Starost of Bydgoszcz, heir of Biskupice, and likewise against the Ląd Convent, heirs of Drzewce — unjustly acknowledging corner mounds also contrary to their former acknowledgements and documents to the Honourable Chełmski — the Honourable Prusimski protests and complains most solemnly before his court.

After the conclusion of the review of the boundary line of the Honourable Chełmski, the Honourable Prusimski, having returned to Studzienka and the hills and bogs called — as they say — the tavern [hills and bogs] described above, and to the corner mound established there, and wishing further to run the second Trąbczyn boundary with Biskupice from the corner mound described above — he requested his court for execution the boundary line in this boundary. The court following began that boundary line in this boundary from the said Studzienka and the corner mound formed there, and going left straight east toward Czarny Bród — went above the tavern hills through the boggy marsh not far from and opposite Studzienka, lying under the hills, overgrown with clumps and various trees. Going through that bog or marshland for about three staj and turning left from the hills, it came out a little onto a hilly and sparse pine. Then further straight east for about two staj, through the very low ground or small drainage strips sparsely overgrown with birch and other trees, it came to the road from the Trąbczyn olędry to the pine forest; and having crossed that road, came again to a not-very-wide bog sparsely overgrown with various trees. Going beside the Cygany mounds lying some distance to the left of the boundary line, it came out onto bare ground of pine forest. From there, going through the bare ground or sparse pine, alongside and beside the eight Cygany mounds and the Lusnia woodland lying a little closer to the boundary line on the left — also eastward a considerable distance through pure pine — it came to the road from Łukomia or Łomowo to Królikowo, and having crossed that road,

went further straight east toward Czarny Bród through pine likewise for about three staj, beside the blazes shown in the review lying close to the boundary line, and came to the beaten road from Szetlewek to Szymanowice to Jarocin. Entering that road and going several staj east toward Czarny Bród through pine, it came to the cross road from Łazy and the Trąbczyn olędry to Gąsiennice and the Otnie bogs — crossing it: first time through the dark-coloured/boggy [czerniawy] drainage strips and pine forests so called; second time further past the road from Łukomia and Łomowo to Królikowo; third time again past the road from Królikowo and Biskupice to Toporów and Gizałki to Tomice, running past Otnie and Gąsiennice. Having crossed that road, it went also east toward Czarny Bród along the same beaten Jarocin road, above the dark-coloured/boggy drainage strip lying on the right side of the Jarocin road, and came to the road from Łazy and the olędry to Królikowo. Having crossed that road, went further also east along the beaten Jarocin road a considerable distance toward Czarny Bród and came to the large mound called Tomal, lying to the left of the road one staj from Czarny Bród — which mound the people hold to be the corner mound of three estates: Łazy, Trąbczyn Wielki, and Biskupice or the deserted lands of Bystrzyce. Beside that mound stand two large pines with crosses cut into them, and a third also with a cross has been felled.

Having stood at that mound, the Honourable Prusimski determined his boundary line and said that here is the confluence and likewise the corner mound — one for the three above-mentioned estates: Trąbczyn Wielki or the deserted [lands] Drzęzgi, Łazy, and Biskupice or the deserted lands of Bystrzyce. From that mound, beginning again his boundary between Trąbczyn Wielki or the deserted [lands] Drzęzgi and Biskupice or the deserted lands of Bystrzyce, he went further along that same beaten Jarocin road east one staj, and came about one staj to the very bank of Czarny Bród — where the court, unable to conclude this boundary due to excessively high water, suspended the further boundary line in it and meanwhile proceeded to further actions on the boundary lines already run.

After the running of the boundary lines and the reviews described above, having concluded them — the present commission court, proceeding to the decision of this cause, and resolving its suspension concerning the unsealing and reading of the sworn inquiries submitted by the Honourable Prusimski — proceeded to unseal and read those sworn inquiries. Having unsealed and read them, since many new circumstances now arise both from the boundary lines and the reviews, and moreover since the Ląd Convent, not appearing here in its court and being treated in default, does not submit its sworn inquiries concerning the corner mounds shown at past sessions, and likewise the Honourable Chełmski does not submit his sworn inquiries — the court, wishing to have the most thorough possible knowledge of this cause and endeavouring to ascertain it in all respects, considers a supplementary sworn inquiry to be necessary.

It orders that the Honourable Prusimski, in proof and counter-proof of his boundary line and that of the Honourable Chełmski, and likewise of the review and the corner mounds and other places in the boundary lines and reviews of both parties shown to his court — draw up and sign interrogatories as thoroughly as possible and submit them to his court for the hearing of knowledgeable, credible, and unexceptionable witnesses — and, taking a preceding oath of non-subornation upon them, introduce those witnesses into his court. The Honourable Prusimski, complying with his court's order, drew up, signed, and submitted the interrogatories to his court. Upon receiving them, his court orders the Honourable Prusimski to take an oath in this formula:

"That the witnesses whom I shall introduce for this sworn inquiry I have neither suborned, nor hired, nor bribed — by anyone or by myself — nor have I engaged them in any other improper manner to the detriment of my neighbour, and I shall not suborn, bribe, or engage them — so help me God and the innocent Passion of His Son."

For the proclamation of that oath,

the Court Summoner assisting this act is assigned. The Honourable Prusimski performed that oath in the formula described above and introduced the witnesses. Thereupon the present commission court, having proceeded to hear the sworn inquiry and having first from each witness individually heard the oath for truthful testimony, conducted those sworn inquiries in the customary manner. After hearing them, and having heard the further controversies of the parties introduced into its court, it proceeded to the resolution and decision of this cause — from the sworn inquiries: two older and the present third, conducted. Having read those sworn inquiries and confronted them with the documents and with the position and layout of places subjected to the required visual inspection as carefully, attentively, and diligently as possible — it proceeded first, as to the resolution of the corner mound at the starting point — as established and formed by both the Honourable Prusimski and the Honourable Chełmski and by the Ląd Convent at several past sessions and now — as ordered to be resolved first by the Tribunal decree.

Since, both from the sworn inquiries read — three in all — and from what was observed on the ground as is customary in a field cause, and likewise from the documents expressed in the schedule by the Honourable Prusimski and signed by the President of his court, and likewise noted in the present act alongside the controversies — it is apparent that although the Honourable Chełmski takes as his greatest foundation for the present cause the judicial site inspection of the late Wojciech Trąbczyński — supposedly heir of all of Trąbczyn — conducted pursuant to the four Konin land decrees between the same Wojciech Trąbczyński as plaintiff on the one side and the late Honourables Tomasz, Jan, and Stanisław Łukomski and Barbara Brodzińska, widow of the late Sebastian Modliboski, co-heirs of Łukomia, summoned separately on the other, of 1589

on Monday before the Feast of Saint Vitus, Martyr — concerning the flooding of the grounds, pine forests, woodlands, meadows, and pastures on the estate of Trąbczyn Wielki and Mały and Osiny by a new pond built on the Łukomia estate — conducted in the field in the same year 1589, on Monday on the vigil of Saint James the Apostle, and attested three years later, in 1592, on Thursday after the Feast of the Immaculate Conception of the Blessed Virgin Mary, in the Konin municipal court — yet to the contrary it is proved to his court by a default decree (default, or the penalty of default) between the same Wojciech, uncle and guardian of his below-named nephews, Wawrzyniec, Walenty, Rachwall, and Stanisław Trąbczyński, co-heirs of Trąbczyn inseparably with him, as plaintiffs on the one side and the Ląd Convent on the other — concerning the flooding of the Trąbczyn meadows by the Tarszew mill, of 1584, on Monday before the Feast of Saint Luke, in the Konin land court — and likewise by three subsequent decrees of 1589 on Monday before the Feast of Saint Vitus in the same Konin land court: namely two, first between the same late Honourable Wojciech, uncle and guardian, and his above-mentioned nephews together and inseparably as plaintiffs on the one side and the late Honourable Mikołaj Trąbczyński, heir of a portion of Szetlew, as defendant on the other, concerning the digging of a ditch and the ploughing up of a road; and a third between those Trąbczyński, uncle and guardian, and his nephews together as plaintiffs and the Ląd Convent, concerning the flooding of the Trąbczyn meadows by the Tarszew mill — namely: that Wojciech Trąbczyński, being not himself sole heir of all of Trąbczyn

or the whole Trąbczyński estate, but together with his above-mentioned nephews, and moreover being their guardian — this judicial site inspection, as expressed above, conducted on the vigil of Saint James in 1589, or in truth only from his own portion, yet to the detriment of his nephews who were minors, contrary to the express law enacted on boundary causes, was made and conducted. Nevertheless he did not have it attested in the municipal court in the same year; but when thereafter in 1591 — as attested by the decree of that year, on Monday after the Sunday Jubilate, in the Konin land court, between his nephews on the one side and the same Wojciech Trąbczyński on the other — he was summoned by his nephews concerning the allocation of the portion of Trąbczyn belonging to them after their father, and the further proceedings on the unconcluded decree or partition continued between them — only then, standing in dispute and lawsuit with his nephews for a year after the decree and three years after the judicial site inspection was conducted in the field — namely in 1592, on Tuesday after the Feast of the Immaculate Conception of the Blessed Virgin Mary, in the Konin municipal court — expressly to the detriment of his nephews — he had it attested.

And moreover it is proved to his court by documents issued that even after this judicial site inspection the further proceedings concerning that partition between those parties continued and were pursued, and those parties remained in dispute and lawsuit — and so both Wojciech Trąbczyński and his nephews were heirs of Trąbczyn each from their portions. And moreover he proves this by the decree of acquittal of 1593 on Monday before

Saint Hedwig — between the same Wojciech, uncle and guardian, and his above-mentioned nephews together as plaintiffs on the one side and the Ląd Convent on the other, concerning the flooding of the Trąbczyn meadows by the Tarszew mill, in the same Konin land court — proving that Wojciech Trąbczyński even after that decree continued to hold his nephews in guardianship and was their guardian and co-heir. And moreover it is adduced to his court by the decree of 1595 on Monday after the Octave of Corpus Christi in the same Konin land court, between the same Wojciech Trąbczyński and Tomasz Łukomski — that Wojciech Trąbczyński, manifestly acting to the detriment of his nephews and in effect in league with the heirs of Łukomia, limited for six years by the Konin land court decree the oath ordered upon the heir of Łukomia — as to how the newly built pond and the holding back of water had caused no flooding and no damage in the grounds, pine forests, and woodlands of Trąbczyn, Nowa Wieś, and Osiny. And moreover he adduces to his court a terminus from the side of the heirs of Trąbczyn — namely Stanisław from Wojciech, Stanisław from Jan, Andrzej from Rachwall, Stanisław from Mikołaj Trąbczyński — against the Ląd Convent, concerning the demarcation of Kopojno, issued in 1616, extracted from the Konin land court records — proving that even after that judicial site inspection and in that year there were several heirs of Trąbczyn.

And finally it is still further proved to his court by the partition of the sixth October 1693 in Trąbczyn, between Andrzej, Jan, and Władysław Trąbczyński, concerning the division of Trąbczyn, Nowa Wieś, Osiny, and Łazy, extracted from the Pyzdry municipal court — that in that year too there was divided ownership in Trąbczyn. And that this divided ownership only then — as attested by the

accounting and the contract between the late Honourable Jan on Kolno Prusimski on the one side and the late Honourables Anna Bojanowska, of her first marriage Trąbczyńska and thereafter Golińska, widow and mother, with Jan and Andrzej, her sons Trąbczyński, in her own name and in the name of her brother Władysław, of 1698, concerning the purchase of those estates, attested in the Poznań municipal court on Monday after Saint Margaret, under pledge of ninety thousand Polish złoty — passed to single ownership. And thereupon, referring to the sworn inquiry and confronting that judicial site inspection together with the sworn inquiries and with other further documents, and likewise with the position of places and from what was observed on the ground — since that judicial site inspection, although conducted in such manner, not by the heir of all but only of a certain portion of Trąbczyn, Wojciech Trąbczyński, and moreover by the guardian of his nephews, then minors in his guardianship and in lawsuit with him at the time of the attestation of this judicial site inspection — past them, to their detriment and expressly by prior arrangement with the heirs of Łukomia, previously arranged, as expressed above — and that privately, by a Court Summoner and nobleman, not by a Boundary Surveyor as was customary at that time, and not by the defendant party but by the plaintiff —

and one might say without any decree at all, since it was not ordered for the plaintiff — is shown not only to be contrary to the Crown statute on minors and on boundary causes, and to the sworn inquiry and to the very corner mounds of the Honourable Chełmski at the starting point and at the terminal point — in short to his entire boundary line, as will be expressed below — but also because the position of the corner mounds of the three estates of Łukom, Trąbczyn, and Drzewce bordering in a corner is shown from nowhere else but from the road which runs to Pyzdry between the estates

of Trąbczyn, Osiny, Łukomia, and the Ląd Convent estate — which words "from the road" can and should rightly be taken and interpreted as nothing other than "before" or "not far from the road", and "between the estates" as nothing other than "between the villages or boundaries". And that road, in the place where the Honourable Chełmski establishes his corner mound supposedly pursuant to that same judicial site inspection — at the head of the Ostrowce woodland — is shown from the sworn inquiry and likewise from what was observed on the ground to be the road that comes from the very village of Osiny and stretches all the way to the beaten Zagórów road, past the corner mound of the Honourable Prusimski, and joins it there — and then crosses through the Zagórów road below the hill and runs to Łukom toward Pyzdry. It is the Osiny road and has been so called from of old; it is not the old Pyzdry road, nor does it go between the estates of Trąbczyn, Osiny, Łukomia, and the Ląd Convent estate — that is Drzewce, Zagórów, and Wrąbczyn, lying beside Łukomia on the right side — where it runs above the Ostrowce woodland [i.e., to the north of the Ostrowce woodland] shown from the sworn inquiry, and above the thirteen Drzewce-Trąbczyn wall mounds [i.e., to the north of them], past the corner mound of the Honourable Prusimski and into the Zagórów road — and then crosses it below the hill and only then runs to Łukom toward Pyzdry. And which even now they call not the Pyzdry road but the Osiny road. And even the Honourable Chełmski himself at three field sessions — as attested by three field decrees: one of 1759 and two of 1760, expressed in the boundary line alongside the controversies from the Oblata records — so called it and so described and expressed it in those decrees and in the protests lodged by the parties at those decrees.

And moreover: that road is not before the corner mounds, nor from nor toward the mounds, but lies between the two corner mounds themselves. And the corner mounds are not from the road, as the judicial site inspection states, but are formed on both sides of the road — contrary to the judicial site inspection.

And moreover it is shown from the sworn inquiry, and from the judicial site inspection of 1644 in the Pyzdry municipal court on Monday post

Dominicam rogationum — conducted by the heirs of Trąbczyn against the heirs of Łukomia in the Ostrowce woodland — and likewise from two default decrees in the same year 1644 in the Konin land court on Monday after the Octave of Corpus Christi, and from two decrees in the Konin court on Monday after Saint Hedwig in the same year 1644, concerning the felling of timber on poles for nets in that woodland, with the Łukomia heirs, with motions to the Tribunal admitted — and likewise from two Piotrków Tribunal decrees of deferral in the same matter, of 1645 on Tuesday before Saint Prisca, from the motions in both cases — that this woodland, at the head or beginning of which the Honourable Chełmski establishes his corner mound, has from of old been called Ostrowce and is proper Trąbczyn property and has always been used and enclosed to Trąbczyn, and the Łukomia estate and its pine forest does not reach here.

Only here is the ground and pine forest — that is the Ostrowce woodland — belonging to Trąbczyn, to the left westward from here; and to the right to Drzewce up to the very mounds — and has been so used from of old both by Trąbczyn and by Drzewce. And those two mounds — #14 and #15 — which the Honourable Chełmski takes as corner mounds, are not corner mounds but are, together with the thirteen stretching from here to the corner mound of the Honourable Prusimski — which Chełmski formerly took and claimed as wall mounds supposedly in a wall between Drzewce and Łukomia — wall mounds between Drzewce and Trąbczyn. And further mounds of the same form are

more numerous in that same wall on the other side of those two mounds, all the way to the Kopojno corner mound. Conversely, from those same sworn inquiries and documents, and likewise from what was observed on the ground, it is shown that the road which the Honourable Prusimski showed in his boundary line actually lies before the three corner mounds — and the mounds lie about one staj from it — is the old Pyzdry road, sufficiently visible and in one place deeply worn above the Zagórów road. And that this road actually runs between the estates or villages of Trąbczyn Mały, Osiny, Drzewce, and further Łukomia and other villages or estates of the Ląd Convent — that is Zagórów, Wrąbczyn, and the ground in them — and likewise past Łukomia (which lies on this side from Zagórów and Drzewce, and has its wall on this side, above the old Pyzdry road, as demarcated by the compromise decree of 1761) — and not toward Łukom as the current Osiny road goes. That road did go there; and nevertheless even now the present road to Pyzdry joins that same old Pyzdry road beyond Łukomia — which cannot be avoided on account of the marshes — and so, joining the old Pyzdry road, reaches Pyzdry itself. This present Osiny road to Pyzdry does not and cannot go between the above-named estates and their grounds, since neither the Drzewce ground nor the Zagórów ground reaches through the Łukomia ground — which lies between both those roads, old and new, taking from the Zagórów road — to this present Osiny road, given that ground and its wall, and cannot reach it; but starting only from the Zagórów road, it runs through the Łukomia ground itself even contrary to the judicial site inspection. And therefore the old Pyzdry road — and likewise the corner mounds, as the judicial site inspection of 1592 states, agreeing only as to this place and showing the position of the corner mound, but not being able to determine boundaries —

are shown here

and proved. And likewise the Zagórów road through Drzewce to Tomice, Kalisz, and Chocz — running beside the corner mounds and called the Zagórów or Drzewce road — and from it the road toward Łukomia branching to the right — are also present and actually found there. And moreover it is shown both from the sworn inquiry and the documents that here in this place at those three corner mounds the ground and pine forest of three estates converge: that of Łukomia, or [as] separated to the Ląd Convent by the compromise decree — to the west, on the right side of the Zagórów or Drzewce road; on the left side of that same Zagórów road and the corner mounds — the Ostrowce woodland, touching on one side here the mounds and on the other all the way to the stawisko further described in the boundary — to the south; and the ground and pine forest of the third estate, Drzewce — to the north. And so they converge from of old, used thus by all those estates.

And there is no separate corner mound or convergence of Bukowe here — which Chełmski at three past sessions said and established was a separate corner mound or convergence here and rejected Trąbczyn — as attested by the field decree of 1760, made in the field between the Ląd Convent and the Honourable Chełmski and filed in the Konin municipal court in 1761 on Monday after the Sunday Jubilate: that it has not been demarcated from time immemorial and is only an estate ground of Łukomia lying within the ground and body of the Łukomia boundaries; it does not exist separately but together with Łukomia — and Bukowe is the same as Łukom. Therefore the convergence here is of Łukomia or Bukowe — and this is attested not only by the present sworn inquiry and the above-mentioned field decree, but also by two further decrees:

one in default or the penalty of default of 1644 on Monday after the Octave of Corpus Christi, and a second from serious controversies with a motion to the Tribunal admitted on Monday after Saint Hedwig in the same year — between the heirs of Trąbczyn and [those of] Łukomia, concerning the demarcation of those

estates — setting forth that besides Bukowe, Łukom, Łomowo, and Imielno are and lie in one ground. And moreover that the road which comes from Zagórów through Drzewce is called both the Zagórów and the Drzewce road, and from it a road to Łukomia also branches — all of which is acknowledged not only by the sworn inquiry but also by the serfs of Drzewce named above: namely the Diligent Tomasz and Kazimierz Czerzniakowie, Tomasz and Fabian, Stefan Głąbik the alderman, Jan Kowal, and Kazimierz Dąbek the cartwright. And finally their very lords — the Ląd Convent — at three sessions conducted from Tribunal decrees, established and acknowledged that same corner mound and mounds and confluence of three estates — Łukomia, Drzewce, and Trąbczyn — not admitting Bukowe but on the contrary rejecting it — bordering their Drzewce wall with Łukomia in that same place, as attested by the above-mentioned field decrees: one of 1759 and two of 1760. And likewise the Honourable Prusimski, heir of Trąbczyn, at that time appearing as a collateral neighbour as to their boundary line, acknowledged and asserted the same as he does now. And moreover the Honourable Chełmski himself acknowledged and said that same corner mound and mounds in this place at all three sessions — with only the difference that he placed Bukowe instead of Trąbczyn here and rejected Trąbczyn. But thereafter, having demarcated in 1761 through a compromise with the Ląd Convent and having abandoned his assertion of Bukowe, he began and demarcated the Łukomia wall with Drzewce instead — and determined it from the Zagórów corner mound, straight opposite and facing the above-described corner mound shown by the Honourable Prusimski, at only one staj — and heaped the last mound in that wall, as attested by the compromise decree of 1761 between him and the Ląd Convent, filed in the Kalisz municipal court on Monday after the Sunday Cantate.

And there at that mound, leaving with his Łukomia wall — which must necessarily

determine at the corner mound of the Honourable Prusimski — only the further Drzewce wall to be taken, which can only run with Trąbczyn and no other estate — he designated this by that same decree, and thereby legitimised and confirmed that same corner mound of the Honourable Prusimski and put aside his assertion of Bukowe. And moreover that corner mound and convergence of the above-mentioned estates — Łukomia, Trąbczyn, and Drzewce — not only the said decrees, not only the sworn inquiry, not only the repeated acknowledgements at past sessions of the Ląd Convent and now of the Drzewce people and of the Honourable Prusimski, and finally of the Honourable Chełmski himself — with only the difference of Bukowe — but also old documents confirm and corroborate [that it lies] nowhere else but at the confluence with Drzewce, and not in that other place where the Honourable Chełmski asserts it to be, but in this very place where the Honourable Prusimski shows it to be and where it ought to be — namely two decrees: one default or the penalty of default, and a second from controversies, against the heirs of Łukomia, principally summoned for the demarcation of Łukomia with Trąbczyn; and two similar ones with Drzewce, concerning the showing of a corner mound and likewise demarcation with them — in the Konin land court of 1644, expressed in the boundary line from the acta et oblata.

Therefore the present commission court, proceeding first to the resolution of the said corner mounds, finds the corner mound of the Honourable Chełmski to be wrong and improper — contrary to all documents and sworn inquiries and even to the very judicial site inspection expressed above — formed on proper Trąbczyn ground at the head or beginning of the woodland called Ostrowce, proved in this place, from two mounds, namely #14 and #15 — wall mounds of Drzewce and likewise of Trąbczyn — and from a third hillock of a stump, and that not as a corner

but actually as a wall mound, and not at the confluence of three estates (since Łukom does not reach there). And at the same time the acknowledgement of this corner mound by the Ląd Convent — made inconsistently and contrary to its former acknowledgements — it finds and holds to be unjust and unwarranted, repudiates, and overturns it.

The corner mound of the Honourable Prusimski, however — as lying at three mounds actually present here in this place, and lying not far from the old Pyzdry road proved here, and being the actual confluence, as a corner, of three estates, namely Łukomia, Drzewce, and Trąbczyn — as visibly shown in conformity with the sworn inquiries, documents, and the position of places and villages — and acknowledged at three sessions by the Ląd Convent and now by the Drzewce people and likewise by the Honourable Prusimski, and finally by the Honourable Chełmski himself — with only the difference of Bukowe — it adjudges, finds, and approves to be good and proper.

[Section 6: Court's Resolution on the Full Boundary Line — Mound-Heaping, Trąbczyn–Łukomia — Oath]

As to the further boundary line of both parties, the route through and the terminal point — since it is shown both from the sworn inquiries and from the documents that the Ostrowce woodland, lying as described above on one side from the corner mounds of the Honourable Prusimski and the fourteen Drzewce-Trąbczyn wall mounds, with the road between those wall mounds and the stawisko running in breadth to the south, and beginning from the supposed corner mound of the Honourable Chełmski already resolved by the court, and stretching in length westward as far as the Zagórów road — is, as attested by the judicial site inspection and proceeding submitted above, proper Trąbczyn property and has been used from of old to Trąbczyn —

and likewise the two mounds going from the corner mounds toward the stawisko — one not far from the corner mounds near the old wolf pit, deep and the other below the hill beside the Osiny — now Pyzdry — road, described in the boundary line, are actual and genuine mounds — and the second mound below the hill, although not so prominent as the first

from the corner mounds, some witnesses reach to it by their memory and observation that it was formerly larger — and that the further boundary of those estates runs along the Zagórów or Kalisz road, which runs beside the corner mounds and beside those two mounds, between the Ostrowce woodland and the pine forest together with the woodland lying to the left of the road in this place, and the Łukomia or convent-separated pine forest lying to the right of the road — running south toward the stawisko and thereafter further beside the stawisko — as acknowledged and asserted by the Honourable Chełmski himself when allotting a portion of pine forest to the Łukomia — to the Ląd Convent for three łany of land by the compromise decree of 1761, made between him and the Ląd Convent, filed in the Kalisz municipal court in the same year on Monday after the Sunday Cantate, and a mound heaped beside that same road as a marker of the pine forest separated — the sworn inquiries and documents attest that it is actual and a boundary road.

And moreover it is shown from the two Trąbczyn partitions — one of 1522 between the Honourables Wojciech and Stanisław, brothers by birth Trąbczyński, heirs of the whole Trąbczyński estate, attested in the Konin municipal court on Thursday in the Octave of the Ascension of the Lord — that the pond with meadows at Nowa Wieś fell to Stanisław Trąbczyński; and that no pond with or without meadows other than this present stawisko, as is apparent from the partition below and from the review of the entire ground Nowej Wsi made for his court, exists at Nowa Wieś. And a second

of the sixth October 1693, between Andrzej, Jan, and Władysław Trąbczyński, heirs of Trąbczyn with appurtenances, dated and made in Trąbczyn and extracted from the Pyzdry municipal court — by which the Honourables Trąbczyński, dividing the Trąbczyn estate, besides the ponds Garmin and Likerowa, reserve to themselves the remaining ponds or rather empty marshes for common fishing, and likewise the flat marshes overgrown with clumps for common use, and likewise that meadows which serfs have long mown should not be taken from them — prove that this stawisko, or rather the empty marshes from that pond depleted over time, belonged from of old to Trąbczyn and Trąbczyn used them, and the serfs — especially from Nowa Wieś and Osiny — had meadows on that stawisko and mowed them; and the grandfather or great-grandfather of the Honourable Chełmski (who once came upon a serf mowing right below the dam itself) did not forbid it and said nothing to him — and indeed, having spoken with him there, rode away. And moreover the four decrees of 1589 expressed above attest this, and the oath ordered by the Konin land court upon the late Tomasz Łukomski, heir of Łukomia — limited for six years in 1595 by the Honourable Wojciech Trąbczyński and thereafter not prosecuted — concerning the flooding of grounds, meadows, and pastures and the drying-out of timber, made and caused on the Trąbczyn Wielki and Mały and Osiny estate by the pond newly built with a dam below the stawisko [i.e., to the west of the stawisko] toward Łukomia on the Łukomia estate by the heirs of Łukomia — and that that flooding was nowhere else but on that stawisko and on those empty marshes — especially since not a little timber, both standing and fallen and dried out, is present on that stawisko.

And moreover that the stawisko or marshes stretch for nearly a mile past Osiny all the way to Czarny Bród and further

to the Królikowo marshes and ponds — and throughout that stretch, and likewise in the place where the Honourable Chełmski ran his boundary line through that stawisko, there is no watershed. And moreover, on the side of that stawisko beyond the Miąsykąt woodland, there are other marshes which are called the duck bogs — and these too, as the sworn inquiry plainly sets forth, were used to Trąbczyn and Łukom did not use or forbid them. And moreover the Honourable Chełmski has below that stawisko [i.e., to the west of it] toward Łukomia a pond — sufficiently large and long — worked on those same marshes on the other side of the Kalisz road, likewise in pine; and, as it is made out of the same kind of marshes as this stawisko, so it is overgrown with clumps and various trees and has only a mill race cut before the mill below the dam, on which pond is a mill and the dam is well made, two staj long, high, and wide — and that pond, having been newly contrived on the marshes, is called the new pond. And it is by that pond and dam, and by holding the water so high, that the flooding of that same stawisko — and of the meadows, pastures, and timber upon it — was caused. Under the pretext of that pond — which the Łukomia heir still has to this day — the heirs of Łukomia, that is the father or grandfather of the Honourable Chełmski, began to encroach upon the said stawisko; and so on it first built a dam of clay and then of timber in the manner of a small weir or sluice in the very middle of the dam — holding back the water so that too much should not go wastefully to his mill — and thereafter encroached also for the meadows. But the heirs of Trąbczyn, that is the predecessors of the Honourable Prusimski — as attested by the numerous judicial site inspections and protests and the decree of 1728 and other acts — resisted and disputed this.

And moreover

it is shown that the Miąsykąt, Dziadulina, and Lusnia woodlands, stretching one after another from that stawisko toward Czarny Bród, always belonged to Trąbczyn and were in use — and as they were proper Trąbczyn property, the uncle of the Honourable Prusimski established olędry upon those woodlands in the time of the father of the Honourable Chełmski — as attested by the rights granted them in 1746 — and the predecessor of the Honourable Chełmski neither resisted nor objected to that settlement, which he would not have omitted had he known those woodlands to be his; or at the very least he would at that time have lodged some manifest or judicial site inspection — none of which the Honourable Chełmski shows in the course of this cause. And moreover it is still shown from those sworn inquiries that the mound immediately beside the pond, above the Zagórów or Kalisz road — after crossing the ditch running from the stawisko toward the Honourable Chełmski's pond — was a sufficiently prominent wall mound which the people saw and remember; and that mound — as the judicial site inspection of 1726 attest — proves to have been ploughed up or scattered by the grandfather of the Honourable Chełmski. And moreover the other mounds above the same Kalisz road are shown to have been damaged and scattered by the former heir of Łukomia, for the ruin of the old boundaries. And moreover it is still apparent from those sworn inquiries that the small field about half a staj beyond that pond — to the left of the boundary line of the Honourable Prusimski and the Zagórów or Kalisz road — on which rather large pine trees now stand, always belonged to and was enclosed by Trąbczyn — for which reason that small field, as is apparent from the very pine trees, has grown up from old.

And moreover it is shown from those sworn inquiries and documents that the old mound described in the boundary line above the small oak, to the right of the Kalisz road, sufficiently round with a small ditch and crown around it, and likewise the other two

above the same Kalisz road to the other side of the road — to the left — described in the boundary line, are genuine and real mounds; and likewise the blazes on old and large pines to the left of the road — and together with the use of [the road], they confirm that the Kalisz road is a boundary. And moreover the dry ford — which is now dry but is now called the Kalisz ford — and the wooden footboards laid upon it, in the branching of the Zagórów or Kalisz road toward Grądzeń, and likewise that same road — and none other — to Grądzeń running west, expressed in Mieleński's judicial site inspection of 1658 — are shown not only from the sworn inquiry but and from what was observed on the ground. And above all it is shown from that very Mieleński judicial site inspection that when the Ląd Convent, demarcating or conducting the judicial site inspection of its Grądzeń ground officially pursuant to a Piotrków Tribunal decree, came to that Kalisz road and at the same time to the Trąbczyn wall above that road — the heir of Trąbczyn, standing there beside that road by reason of his boundary, held that same Kalisz road to be a boundary, asserted the ground and pine forest to the left of that road as his Trąbczyn property and that to the right as Grądzeń's — and the Convent acknowledged the same to him.

Yet the predecessor of the Honourable Chełmski, heir of Łukomia, protested against him at that judicial site inspection — not that he was acknowledging the Kalisz road as a boundary, but only that he was attributing the wall not to Łukomia but to Grądzeń. By which that

predecessor of the Honourable Chełmski, having at that time better knowledge than now, acknowledged the Kalisz road and did not deny it. As to the further boundary line in this boundary, running above the duck bogs [i.e., to the north of the duck bogs] — since it is shown that the duck bogs, as expressed above, belong and belonged to Trąbczyn and Trąbczyn used them up to the time of the delivery of possession. And moreover the forked pine is stated to stand on an actual mound and the mound under it is to be found there. And moreover the sworn inquiries assert and show the further boundary line in that boundary and its continuity — and weighing the continuity — since not only the sworn inquiries but also the documents expressed above in the boundary line, namely the proceedings and decrees and the oath with the heirs of Biskupice concerning the private nocturnal heaping of eight mounds and the making of forty-one blazes on trees, attest that the eight mounds called Cygany are heaped privately and by night on Trąbczyn ground, at no small expense and detriment to the Trąbczyn ground — but the mounds, taking from the first, lying about one and a half staj from the boundary line of the Honourable Prusimski and the Kalisz road beside it, and the Lusnia woodland enclose and show still more of the further boundary line and continuity of the Trąbczyn boundary and ground, and repel the boundary line of the Honourable Chełmski.

And finally, since the corner mound or confluence of the three estates — Trąbczyn, Łukomia, and Biskupice — and the determination of the terminal point as to this boundary, at Studzienka and the tavern hills and bogs lying by that Studzienka — and moreover that Studzienka itself, faced with brick and demolished by the Honourable Chełmski so that people going on pilgrimage to Zagórów, not finding water in that well, might resort to his inn — which nonetheless still exists, though without facingwork — and likewise the cross on the pine opposite that Studzienka, above the very road, and likewise three corner mounds said to be there,

are shown to be there; and finally that the tavern hills and bogs lying there and subject to the court's inspection, holding their name from the inn once in the pine nearby belonging to Królikowo or Białły — the ruins of which also were shown to the court — are shown not only by the sworn inquiry but also by Mieleński's judicial site inspection of 1658. And moreover the blazes on various pines above the Kalisz road and the use of the pine forest up to this point — and further, as some witnesses assert — are maintained by Trąbczyn and proved and shown.

Since from those sworn inquiries and the above-mentioned documents it is shown that the Honourable Chełmski first established a corner mound on Trąbczyn ground, and then beginning from his corner mound — already resolved and adjudged by its court — took in his boundary line the entire Ostrowce woodland, and half the stawisko or rather empty marshes, and the Miąsykąt, Dziadulina, and Lusnia woodlands, and nearly all the fields and meadows of the olędry, and the twelve olędry themselves and the inn — belonging to Trąbczyn from of old as has been shown above — and used by them. And moreover a few years before the unlawful and improper delivery of possession, in 1771, he took possession of them — and that whole boundary line, as at the starting point improperly begun, so through and to the terminal point run through the proper grounds, fields, and woodlands of Trąbczyn. And he took half the stawisko, larger, supposedly to a watershed — of which there is none there and is not apparent and cannot be — since the stawisko or marshes extend uniformly for a mile in all directions. And the Miąsykąt woodlands — of which

there is no mention whatsoever in the judicial site inspection of 1589 made in the field and attested in 1592 — he took on some unknown foundation. And moreover the hillocks which he showed in his boundary line as mounds are shown from the sworn inquiry and likewise from what was observed on the ground during the review of his boundary line — and from the judicial site inspection of this year conducted in the boundary line — attested in the Konin municipal court on Tuesday before the Feast of Saint Francis by the Court Summoner and nobleman — that the mounds shown and named as such in the boundary and at the corner mound formed at the Lusnia woodland are not mounds but hillocks or stumps, and many more of the same kind and in great number are found in the pine forest. And finally, since both the sworn inquiry and the proceedings and decrees with the heirs of Biskupice — both in the Kalisz land court at the Konin sessions and in the Tribunal — concerning the heaping of eight mounds and the making of forty-one blazes above the Lusnia, and the swearing-off ordered upon Biskupski with two witnesses of equal birth, performed by him — expressed above in the controversy at the review — show that those eight mounds and blazes are on Trąbczyn ground, and with great expense and detriment to the Trąbczyn ground above the Lusnia woodland [i.e., to the north of the Lusnia woodland] beginning from the corduroy crossings, privately and by night — and for that reason they are called Cygany.

And those mounds and blazes are actually — as is apparent from the sworn inquiry and the court's own review — above the Lusnia woodland [i.e., to the north of it], about

one staj from the woodland, and stretch toward the corduroy crossings — which corduroy crossings, as attested by the sworn inquiry, lie not far from Czarny Bród — and moreover they enclose the corner mound of the Honourable Chełmski and extend still further toward the corduroy crossings; and the heir of Biskupice swore off those mounds and blazes, saying that he had not heaped nor made them — and so it is apparent from this that he had not usurped to himself the ground enclosed by those mounds and blazes — extending more than one staj from the Lusnia woodland in breadth toward the mounds — and likewise the Lusnia woodland; and even if he had usurped, and through that usurpation had made those mounds and blazes, yet having sworn off those mounds and blazes and renounced their effects, he has already renounced and sworn off both the ground on which those mounds and blazes stand and that which lies between the mounds and the Lusnia woodland.

And it follows from this that he has already acknowledged and shown that ground to be Trąbczyn's; and since those mounds and blazes were made at great expense and detriment to the Trąbczyn ground, therefore they show and prove not only the title and ownership of that ground on which they stand, and of that which lies between them and the Lusnia woodland, to belong to Trąbczyn, but also the entirety of the Lusnia woodland and moreover the further continuity and breadth of the Trąbczyn ground — which the mounds and blazes themselves, or rather their actual and obvious existence, clinch and demonstrate — and by the same token do not permit Biskupice to reach the Lusnia woodland and to the alleged corner mound of the Honourable Chełmski, nor any convergence with Łukomia and Trąbczyn at the Lusnia

woodland; and on the contrary, they assert and maintain that the ground and pine forest lying between those Cygany mounds and blazes and the Lusnia woodland — from the corduroy crossings all the way to the Kalisz road, and to the Kalisz road itself (since, taking from the corduroy crossings, the last Cygany mound lies near the Kalisz road, as is apparent from the first review) — must extend and reach to Trąbczyn. And that this is so is proved and demonstrated: that the ground lying between the Lusnia woodland and the mounds and blazes, together with the entire Lusnia woodland, and likewise that on which the mounds and blazes stand — and still further, in breadth and in length beyond the mounds — has without question been used; and it is thus openly and demonstrably apparent that neither the Honourable Chełmski a corner mound, nor the Honourable Gałecki, Starost of Bydgoszcz, heir of Biskupice, his convergence, can or should have here in this place above the Lusnia woodland [i.e., to the north of it] as they claim, pursuant to the judicial site inspection mentioned so many times.

For if the corner mound and convergence of the Biskupice estates were to be found here in this place, then all the ground and pine forest lying between the Lusnia woodland and the Cygany mounds and blazes, and likewise that on which the Cygany mounds and blazes stand — and still further in breadth to the south and in length to the east, belonging to Trąbczyn — on one side from the corner mound, east toward the corduroy crossings and Czarny Bród, the heir of Biskupice, and on the other side from that same corner mound, west toward the Kalisz road, the heir of Łukomia — together with the Cygany mounds and blazes, in Trąbczyn's own proper ground as proved by sworn inquiry and documents — and still further, a large portion of the Trąbczyn ground and

pine forest in breadth and in length — both would take away, and both would divide between themselves another's Trąbczyn ground, and between themselves would divide the boundary of the Honourable Prusimski confirmed by so many proofs — which is shown and proved to his court openly and actually not only from the sworn inquiry, not only from the documents, the arguments, and the party's exposition, but also from the position and layout of the places and of the Cygany mounds so called and the blazes expressed above, and from what was observed on the ground. And finally, since it appears that the boundary of the Honourable Prusimski is confirmed — by the documents, by the sworn inquiry, by the ownership of the woodlands, and notably Ostrowce, the stawisko, Dziadulina, Miąsykąt, Lusnia — by the use of them, by actual mounds and corner mounds, by the boundary road Kalisz or Zagórów, by Studzienka and the tavern hills and bogs, and by the other markers and blazes shown in the boundary line and other positions of places, and finally by the actual position and existence of the Cygany mounds, and by the settlement of the olędry as so long established and genuine, confirmed by documents and sworn inquiries and by the position and location of places, and finally in agreement with Crown law —

then the Honourable Chełmski, the boundary wall of Trąbczyn standing in the way, legitimately proved and adduced, in no way can — pursuant to the judicial site inspection of 1592 — reach Biskupice, nor have a corner mound here in this place at the Lusnia woodland, nor can they converge there; and otherwise pursuant to that judicial site inspection he can reach Biskupice only necessarily through Trąbczyn's own ground — just as the Honourable Chełmski now does. And that this is so is shown by the later judicial site inspection of 1593,

conducted on Thursday on the very Feast of Saint Mary Magdalene, from the side of the Łukomia heirs, in that those same persons — going, or rather leading the mounds to Biskupice — wishing to accomplish this could not do so except by way of the Osiny boundaries — which are the same as the Trąbczyn boundaries. And accordingly, those and such proofs, sworn inquiries, documents, and finally the Cygany mounds and blazes and all other arguments and circumstances being weighed — since not only the above-mentioned judicial site inspection but also the present boundary between Trąbczyn and Łukomia as run by the Honourable Chełmski, and his corner mound, are shown to be at variance with all those proofs, and this is proved to his court —

therefore the present commission court, proceeding by virtue of the constitution or commission issued by the Estates of the Commonwealth for the full adjudication of the present cause and the weighing of documents — and considering and observing the proofs expressed above, which are without any doubt, and on the contrary demonstrate and confirm the reality of the ancient boundaries and the boundary line of the Honourable Prusimski; and likewise the Crown law established in boundary causes, which orders the Sub-chamberlain court to observe and maintain the greater probability in boundaries, and to admit the party demonstrating the better improvement and the more notable markers to the oath upon honour and conscience — and with respect to the said judicial site inspection, as conducted by the heir of only a portion and not the whole of Trąbczyn, during a guardianship held over his nephews who were also co-heirs of Trąbczyn along with him, without them, and on the contrary, a dispute and discord standing

between them — and that only from the portion of one heir, past the other heirs — and with prejudice to them, expressly by prior arrangement with the heirs of Łukomia previously arranged, contrary to the law of nature and the Crown law, and contrary to the proofs expressed above and to the ancient boundaries confirmed by so many documents and mounds as now proved by the Honourable Prusimski, and contrary to the layout of places and villages — conducted; and contrary to all documents issued before and after it expressed above, and to the sworn inquiry, and finally contrary to the Honourable Chełmski's own boundary line and corner mounds; and moreover as ordered to be maintained only insofar as it was not contrary to other documents, by the last decree of the Bydgoszcz Tribunal of 1768; and as being unable to cause prejudice and harm to other heirs, the pledgors and antecessors of the Honourable Prusimski, as not entering into it, and moreover then minors in the guardianship of the said Wojciech Trąbczyński, and — as is apparent from the partition deed of 1693 — remaining in possession of the stawisko or rather of the empty marshes, not secured by that same judicial site inspection; and as unable to establish new boundaries against them — not regarding it,

and on the contrary dismissing and setting aside it as unlawful and invalid, condemned by law itself — it dismisses and sets aside the boundary line of the Honourable Chełmski: both as to the corner mound at the starting point, already resolved, and as to the wall and the terminal point — as run through his own proper ground, pine forests, woodlands, the stawisko or empty marshes, likewise not present in that judicial site inspection — such as Miąsykąt and Dziadulina —

and as run not by way of the watershed of the stawisko but straight through the centre of the stawisko or marshes, contrary to the documents, sworn inquiries, mounds, and other genuine boundary markers expressed in the boundary line of the Honourable Prusimski, and contrary to law and ancient use — run contrary and unlawfully, unsupported by any documents or boundary markers — recognises as unjust, unlawful, and inequitable, and rejects and sets it aside entirely — both at the starting point and through and to the terminal point. And the boundary line of the Honourable Prusimski, as run and proved in accordance with the sworn inquiries and documents both written and standing on the ground, and confirmed with the survey, and supported by genuine mounds, blazes, and other markers, and by ancient use — together with the corner mound expressed and resolved above, both at the starting point and through and to the terminal point — described already both in the boundary walk and in its sentence above — in all and in everything — it approves, confirms, and adjudges to be the better, and for the greater security thereof, pursuant to the statute's disposition, orders the Honourable Prusimski to swear on the last and final mound heaped above, with six witnesses of equal birth, in the formula prescribed by the statute below, after the mounds have been heaped.

And not having any available — on account of the intervening death of the late Honourable Antoni Wolski, Sub-chamberlain of Inowłocław, and of Bartłomiej Kraszewski, his Inowłocław Boundary Surveyor, appointed pursuant to the constitution of the Commonwealth together with him to this cause — commissioners to heap mounds together from the Sub-chamberlain office, and preserving the rights and the Sub-chamberlain function —

therefore in such circumstances the present commission court, having the power granted it by the Commonwealth for the needs and circumstances of the case in this cause to establish what is necessary and to adjudicate it sufficiently and finally — not regarding also the non-appearance of any party — orders that the Honourable Prusimski, pursuant to the court's judicial declaration issued to him during the writing of the sentence or decree, bring in the Sub-chamberlain office, that is the Kalisz Boundary Surveyor; and that the Boundary Surveyor, once brought in, in the presence of his court shall heap the corner and wall mounds pursuant to the sentence or decree of his court — leaving old mounds and blazes untouched and indeed retaining them for the security and substantive confirmation of his court's judgment — and heaping new ones beside the old ones in the boundary line of the Honourable Prusimski, and shall fully mark out those boundaries with mounds and other legally prescribed markers. After those mounds have been heaped, he shall administer the oath on the last and final mound — together with his court — from the Honourable Prusimski and his six witnesses equal to him in birth, pursuant to the statute formula.

The Honourable Prusimski, complying with his court's order, since he has brought in the Honourable Wojciech Czarnecki, Kalisz Boundary Surveyor — the Honourable Czarnecki, Kalisz Boundary Surveyor, for the execution of his court's decree pursuant to its provisions, proceeded to heap mounds in the boundary line between Trąbczyn and Łukomia, in the presence of his court, or rather together with his court.

And so, beginning the heaping of mounds from the corner mound

or confluence of three estates — Trąbczyn, Łukomia, and Drzewce — and having set out a measuring staff of eight łokci for the measurement of mounds, and having placed the boundary markers — namely several whole bricks, slag, glass, stones, and charcoal — at the places of those mounds, and having applied at the place of the Trąbczyn mound a glass bottle with the description of the commission — he heaped three corner mounds as a corner, one from another in equal measure lying close together beside the old corner mounds left intact just as they were — emerging from their centre, not far from the old Pyzdry road, about one staj before the mounds to the north toward Drzewce, as shown from the sworn inquiry. And describing now more precisely the location of those mounds — namely between three roads: one, the old Pyzdry road, lying above and not far before them; a second, from Zagórów township through Drzewce to Kalisz, Chocz, and Tomice, running beside the mounds — called the Zagórów and also the Drzewce road; and a third, the Osiny road, running from Osiny through the pine forest above the fourteen Drzewce-Trąbczyn wall mounds past the above-described corner mounds and joining the Zagórów road, and then crossing through the Zagórów road below the hill and running now to Łukom toward Pyzdry — which three mounds divide the above-mentioned estates: Łukom to the west, Trąbczyn to the south, Drzewce to the north, and their pine forests.

From those corner mounds, going a little to the right, somewhat past south diagonally — and crossing the Zagórów or Drzewce new road to Kalisz, Chocz, and Tomice — twenty-two pręty along the old Zagórów road: the first wall mound heaped — beside which is the old mound left intact, well prominent, and the wolf pit to the

right of it — where also beside that mound and the wolf pit to the right is a road not very prominent branching from the Zagórów or Drzewce road to the right above, joining the Osiny — now Pyzdry — road by a shortcut below the hill and running together with the Osiny — now Pyzdry — road beside the hill toward Łukomia and to Łukomia. And to the left a pine with a cross blaze newly cut.

Likewise going further along the old road — thirteen pręty — the second wall mound heaped on that same road.

Likewise going further — the third wall mound, after crossing the new road from Osiny to Pyzdry on the right side of the Zagórów road below the sandy hill — heaped thirteen pręty away. Beside that mound is the old mound, not very prominent but round, shown from the sworn inquiry, and an old pine stump stands upon it; and a cross blaze on a pine newly made.

Likewise the fourth wall mound going south, to the left of the Zagórów or Drzewce or Kalisz road — twenty-five pręty — heaped.

The fifth wall mound to the right of that same road — twenty-five pręty — heaped.

The sixth wall mound to the left of that same Zagórów or Kalisz road, not far from the Ląd Convent mound as a marker of the separated [pine forest] to the right of that road — twenty-five pręty — heaped.

The seventh wall mound not far from the dam at the stawisko, to the right of that same road — likewise twenty-five pręty — heaped.

The eighth wall mound after crossing the filled-in ditch at the dam of that stawisko, to the right of the old Zagórów

or Kalisz road — twenty-five pręty — heaped, and that mound is surrounded with hornbeam.

The ninth wall mound to the right of that same road, on the small field or strip above the Łukomia meadows [i.e., to the north of the Łukomia meadows] — likewise twenty-five pręty — heaped.

The tenth wall mound likewise to the right of that same road, at the end of that small field or strip — likewise twenty-five pręty — heaped; beside that mound is the old mound to its right, and a cross blaze worked in an oak.

The eleventh wall mound to the right of that same road — likewise twenty-five pręty — heaped.

The twelfth mound to the right of that same road, on bare ground — likewise twenty-five pręty — heaped.

The thirteenth wall mound to the left of that same road — twenty-five pręty — heaped; beside that mound is an old blaze, well prominent, cut trough-fashion on a pine.

The fourteenth wall mound to the left of that same road, in low ground — twenty-five pręty, close to the hill to the right of that road — heaped; beside that mound is also an old blaze cut on a pine.

The fifteenth wall mound to the left of that same road — likewise twenty-five pręty — heaped.

The sixteenth wall mound to the left of the old road, not far from the drainage strip stretching from the said stawisko — twenty-five pręty, and not far from the Łomowo field — heaped.

The seventeenth wall mound to the right of that same Zagórów or Kalisz road, beside the cross road from Łukomia to the Trąbczyn olędry, on bare ground above the low ground [i.e., to the north of the low ground] — heaped.

The eighteenth wall mound to the right of the old Zagórów or

Drzewce road to Kalisz, Chocz, and Tomice, as described above, beside the cross road from Łukomia and Łomowo to Królikowo — likewise twenty-five pręty, on bare ground opposite the Łomowo field lying some tens of pręty away — heaped; beside that mound is a well-prominent old mound and a cross newly cut on a pine to the left.

The nineteenth wall mound to the right of that same old road, going south, not far from the new Łomowo clearings — likewise twenty-five pręty — heaped.

The twentieth wall mound to the right of the road below the small hill, not far from those same new Łomowo clearings — likewise twenty-five pręty — heaped.

The twenty-first wall mound to the left of that same Kalisz road and to the right of the second road from Łomowo to Królikowo, beyond the drainage strip touching the Kalisz road at its end — not far from the new Łomowo clearings, on which the stumps still stand, twenty-five pręty — heaped.

The twenty-second wall mound to the right of the Kalisz road beside the very Kalisz ford — likewise twenty-five pręty, close to the new Łomowo clearings — heaped.

The twenty-third wall mound to the left of the Kalisz road — that is going left southward and leaving to the right the road from Zagórów, Drzewce, Łomowo, and Łukomia past Grądzeń to Tomice running west — and running left southward along the Kalisz road, immediately after the ford — likewise twenty-five pręty — heaped to the left of that road; beside that mound is one old and one new cross blaze cut on a pine, and opposite that blaze a small icon is cut into a pine.

[Translator's note: Obrazek w sosnę wcięty: "a small icon cut into a pine". Religious wayside images carved into roadside trees were a common vernacular devotional practice in early modern Poland. Translated literally; the precise form is not specified.]

The twenty-fourth mound

wall mound between the old and the new Kalisz road, beside the low ground — twenty-five pręty — heaped.

The twenty-fifth wall mound likewise at the very branching of the Kalisz and old Tomice roads, the latter branching right from the beaten Kalisz road toward Tomice toward the small mill — likewise twenty-five pręty — heaped.

The twenty-sixth wall mound to the right of the old Kalisz road, going south, a little before the cross beaten road from Trąbczyn — formerly through pine and now through the Trąbczyn olędry — to Tomice, beside the Pyzdry corner mounds lying a half-mile west from the Kalisz road toward Tomice — and joining the first above-mentioned Tomice road a few staj above below the hill — likewise twenty-five pręty — heaped; beside that mound is a new and an old blaze on a pine.

The twenty-seventh wall mound to the right of the old Kalisz road — twenty-five pręty, in pine — heaped.

The twenty-eighth wall mound likewise above that old Kalisz road in pine — heaped; beside that mound a cross newly made on a pine to the right of the old Kalisz road.

The twenty-ninth wall mound likewise — twenty-five pręty — heaped.

The thirtieth wall mound to the right of the old Kalisz road, beyond the small ford and not far from the road from the olędry to Szymanowice crossing through the Kalisz road — between the drainage strips stretching from the duck bogs on both sides of the Kalisz road — likewise twenty-five pręty — heaped.

The thirty-first wall mound going south to the left of the Kalisz road, above the drainage strip stretching from the duck bogs [i.e., to the north of it] — twenty-five pręty — heaped.

The thirty-second wall mound to the right of the road, not far from the bogs stretching from the duck bogs

— likewise twenty-five pręty — heaped; beside that mound a new cross cut on a pine.

The thirty-third wall mound to the right of the old road, on bare ground — likewise heaped.

The thirty-fourth wall mound going south above the small porowa [i.e., to the north of it] crossing through that road — beside which is the forked pine standing on the mound, with old blazes upon it well prominent — twenty-five pręty, to the left of the Kalisz road — heaped.

The thirty-fifth wall mound to the left of that same Kalisz road — likewise twenty-five pręty — heaped.

The thirty-sixth wall mound to the right of that same road, in low ground, at the same distance — heaped.

The thirty-seventh wall mound to the right of the Kalisz road, on bare ground — twenty-five pręty — heaped.

The thirty-eighth wall mound to the left of the road — beside which a cross is cut on a pine — thirty pręty — heaped.

The thirty-ninth wall mound going south toward Studzienka, to the right of the road — thirty pręty — heaped; opposite that mound, about one and a half staj west from the Kalisz road, lies the first of the eight mounds called Cygany, in low ground.

The fortieth, likewise heaped on bare ground.

The forty-first to the right of that same road — likewise thirty pręty, on bare ground — heaped.

The forty-second to the left of that same road — thirty pręty — likewise heaped.

The forty-third going south to the right of that same road — likewise thirty pręty — likewise heaped.

The forty-fourth

wall mound to the right of that same Kalisz road, in low ground — thirty pręty;

And from Studzienka and the tavern hills and bogs lying there — eighteen pręty — heaped.

All those mounds described above in order are wall mounds and divide the estates and pine forests: to the left of the Kalisz road — Trąbczyn Mały or Nowa Wieś; to the right — Łukomia or Łomowo.

And in those mounds are placed the same markers [znaki] as in the corner mounds, except for the bottle. After those mounds had been heaped, having come to Studzienka and the hills stretching crosswise through the Kalisz road and likewise the drainage strips on the far side of the hills or beyond the hills — called the tavern hills and bogs, described above in the boundary line — and having placed similar markers as in the other mounds at the places designated for mounds — he heaped three corner mounds as a corner, one from another in equal measure lying close together, emerging from their centre — dividing the three estates at the corner point itself here: Łukom or Łomowo, or the present Rozciszew folwark; Trąbczyn Mały or Nowa Wieś; and Biskupice. Two to the left of the Kalisz road — one for Trąbczyn to the left and one for Biskupice to the right of Studzienka below the hill — and the third for Łukomia to the right of the Kalisz road likewise below the hill — heaped. At those corner mounds are

[Translator's note: znaki, in Old Polish: A notch in a tree or an artificial mound to mark the boundary separating two areas belonging to two different owners, or the boundary itself. (https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/znak#Old_Polish)]

two crosses on two pines — one old and burnt, and on the other newly cut.

Likewise not far from those corner mounds on the far side of the hills — through which the Kalisz road passes between the mounds — between the bogs is the large stone, three łokci long, lying crosswise beside the very Kalisz road, not very raised above the ground, which stands as custodian of the corner mounds; beside that stone is the road branching right from the Kalisz road toward Rozciszew, running above the hill.

Having thus heaped those mounds, the said commission court and likewise the Sub-chamberlain office — that is the above-mentioned Boundary Surveyor — orders that the Honourable Prusimski, complying both with the Crown law and with the sentence of his court, shall swear on his last and final mound together with six witnesses equal to him in birth, in the formula prescribed by the statute: "As I have rightly and justly made this present demarcation by mounds and this boundary line, and have taken nothing whatsoever from the other party's ground by this present demarcation and mounds, nor have I wronged my neighbour."

For the proclamation of that oath and formula, the loyal Court Summoner Bartłomiej of Trąbczyn, assisting this present act, is assigned.

The Honourable Prusimski, complying therewith both with the Crown law and with the sentence of his court, performed that oath with six witnesses possessing their own estates — namely the Honourables Józef Pawłowski, Antoni Lipiński, Stefan Waliszewski, Jan Zdanowski, Jan Bagiński, and Piotr Makowiecki — on the image of Christ the Lord crucified, in the formula described above, on the last final (axialny) Trąbczyn mound, in the presence of many worthy persons.

[Translator's note: Acjalny / axialny: the source gives acjalny in Roman type and axialny in square brackets, indicating the transcriber's reading of what is presumably the same word — the Latin/polonised axialis, meaning "final" or "axial" (the last and decisive mound in the series upon which the oath is taken). Retained as "final" in the text; the dual form is flagged here.]

After that performance, having acknowledged the compliance with the sentence of his court, the court wishes to hold and confirms this present demarcation, marked by mounds and other signs, as inviolable; and ordered the Court Summoner to proclaim it.

[Section 7: Court's Resolution on the Trąbczyn–Biskupice Boundary — Mound-Heaping — Damages — Annulment of the Delivery of Possession — Default Judgement — Signatory Block]

After the mounds — both corner and wall mounds — had been heaped in the boundary between Trąbczyn Mały or Nowa Wieś and Łukomia or Łomowo, and after the oath had been performed by the Honourable Prusimski and his witnesses on the last Trąbczyn mound, the present commission court, having suspended for a time its deliberations concerning the delivery of possession and the proceedings and the return of the income from the olędry, and likewise concerning the claims for

transgressions and damages arising from the now demarcated boundary — proceeding to the resolution of the second boundary, between Trąbczyn or Nowa Wieś and Biskupice, or rather of the boundary line run by the Honourable Prusimski in that boundary in absentia, as ordered to be finally adjudged by the constitution or present commission even in default — having made and heard the controversies and proceeded to read the sworn inquiry, and having read it as diligently as possible, and having confronted it with the documents and with the position and layout of places subjected to the required visual inspection — since it is shown both from the documents submitted at the review of the eight mounds and blazes of the same act and year expressed above, and from the sworn inquiry subjected to the required visual inspection that although the eight mounds and forty-one blazes are lying above the Lusnia woodland [i.e., to the north of the Lusnia woodland], about one staj — and in some places more — from that woodland, and stretching one and a half staj from the Kalisz road toward the corduroy crossings and Czarny Bród to the east — they are actually present and shown at

the review; and those mounds and blazes — both from the documents, namely the judicial site inspection of 1644 in the Pyzdry municipal court on Saturday before the Feast of the Holy Trinity by the Trąbczyn heirs against the Biskupice heirs together with the protest concerning the heaping of those mounds and blazes, and the default decree and two decrees from controversies with motions to the Crown Tribunal of the same year, and likewise two Piotrków Tribunal decrees of deferral in 1645, and the third Tribunal decree of 1646 concerning the heaping of those mounds and blazes against the Biskupice heirs, and finally the Konin land court decree of 1647 from the Tribunal decree and the oath performed thereat by the Honourable Biskupski — all described more fully in the review — and likewise from the present sworn inquiries: are shown to have been heaped privately and by night on proper Trąbczyn ground, at great expense and detriment to the Trąbczyn heirs, above the Lusnia woodland [i.e., to the north of it] beginning from the corduroy crossings; and for that reason they are called Cygany.

And so those mounds and blazes themselves show the further continuity and breadth of the Trąbczyn ground — and this continuity of the Trąbczyn ground, taking it from the Kalisz road and past those mounds and blazes toward the corduroy crossings, as shown from the sworn inquiry not far from Czarny Bród, from which the blazes and mounds began to be heaped there toward the corduroy crossings; and further toward the Biskupice corner mound with Trąbczyn and Łazy — lying one staj from Czarny Bród beside the beaten road from Szetlewek to Jarocin — and to that same corner mound and to Czarny Bród it is shown from the sworn inquiry to reach. And as to the other side — the breadth of that Trąbczyn ground to the south of those mounds — although the Honourable Prusimski does not prove in his court by any documents to which estate it extends and with which estate it meets there — nor the judicial site inspection upon those mounds and blazes ordered by the Tribunal decrees, from which the last Tribunal decree of 1646, ordering the performance of the enjoined oath, issued; nor the second judicial site inspection of

1655 by Głoskowski, Kalisz Boundary Surveyor, expressed in the two Bydgoszcz Tribunal decrees — nor any proceeding or field decree nor earlier summons — yet from the sworn inquiries alone, not only the present boundary line but still further — past and beyond the boundary line — toward Otnie and Gąsiennice or Myszynice, as some witnesses testified and attested the further use of the ground beyond Studzienka, it is apparent.

And moreover that the heir of Biskupice, Biskupski — although it is not definitively established that he was to stand at Studzienka — yet was to stand at the Kalisz road and establish his boundaries there; and that the Biskupice ground or his convergence is to be found here at Studzienka — is shown from the sworn inquiry. And moreover that the large mound above [i.e., to the north of] the road from Szetlewek through Czarny Bród to Jarocin, lying about one staj before Czarny Bród, beside which are two pines with crosses and a third felled likewise with a cross, is an actual corner mound — one for three — and there the estates of Łazy, Trąbczyn Wielki or the deserted lands Drzęzgi, and Biskupice or the deserted lands of Bystrzyce converge.

And there in that place and further to Czarny Bród from that corner mound they hold the Szetlew road as a boundary and peacefully use the ground and pine forest — Trąbczyn and Biskupice each on their side. Therefore the present commission court, weighing those proofs, adjudges, assigns, approves, confirms, and ratifies the full and entire boundary line of the said Honourable Prusimski, Starost of Niszczewice — both from the starting point and through and to the terminal point — that is to the above-mentioned corner mound lying one staj before Czarny Bród — and likewise the boundary line in the third boundary from that corner mound between Trąbczyn Wielki or the deserted lands Drzęzgi and Biskupice or the deserted lands of Bystrzyce — from the corner mound to Czarny Bród, run for one staj, unable to be determined on account of excessively high water — as legitimately run to that point — to the Honourable Prusimski, Starost of Niszczewice. And for the greater security thereof, pursuant to the statute's disposition, orders him to swear on the last corner mound described and heaped above, with six witnesses of equal birth, in the formula prescribed by the statute — after the mounds have been heaped. And returning to the corner mound established at Studzienka in the boundary of Trąbczyn Mały or Nowa Wieś, it proceeds to heap mounds together with the above-mentioned Honourable Wojciech Czarnecki, Kalisz Boundary Surveyor.

And beginning that heaping of mounds toward the east — the said Boundary Surveyor, having measured twelve eight-łokieć pręty from the corner mounds and Studzienka to the boggy marsh and having placed the same markers as in the other mounds at the designated mound place —

[Translator's note: The commission's definition of the pręt: The source at this point reads dwanaście prętów ośm łokciowych — "twelve pręty of eight łokci" — where ośm łokciowych directly qualifies prętów, defining each pręt used in this survey as an eight-łokieć unit. This is confirmed by an earlier passage (the review of the Drzewce–Trąbczyn wall mounds, f. 680v), where inter-mound distances are stated in łokci and the phrase jako się z wymiaru laską osiem łokciową pokazuje — "as shown by measurement with an eight-łokieć staff" — makes explicit that the commission's measuring instrument throughout was an eight-łokieć staff, one length of which constitutes one pręt in this record. The standard Polish pręt of the period was 15 łokci; the commission's 8-łokieć pręt is therefore a local working unit specific to this survey. Using the standard 18th-century Polish łokieć of approximately 59.5 cm, one commission pręt ≈ 4.76 m. The most frequent mound interval in the record (25 pręty) thus corresponds to approximately 119 m; 30 pręty ≈ 143 m; 35 pręty ≈ 167 m.]

Heaped the first mound, lying east from the above-described corner mound before the very bog at the beginning of that boundary of Trąbczyn Mały or Nowa Wieś with Biskupice below the hill.

And from that mound, going further along that same boggy and boundary-line-cut marsh straight east, the second mound is heaped — thirty pręty — in which three stakes one łokieć above the mound are driven, and boundary markers — stones and brick only — are placed.

Likewise going further — the third mound in that same bog — thirty pręty — and three stakes in it;

The fourth mound and three stakes in it in that same bog — likewise with stakes — heaped.

The fifth mound, coming out a little onto drier though still low ground — likewise thirty pręty, without stakes — heaped.

The sixth mound above the boggy ground — thirty pręty — heaped.

The seventh mound and three stakes in it, on bog — likewise thirty pręty — heaped.

The eighth, ninth, tenth, eleventh, twelfth, thirteenth, fourteenth, fifteenth,

sixteenth, seventeenth mounds in a straight line going east, on bare ground — each thirty pręty — heaped.

The eighteenth mound on bare ground below the hill — likewise thirty pręty — heaped.

The nineteenth mound on bare ground on a small hill — likewise thirty pręty — heaped.

The twentieth mound — likewise thirty pręty — not far from the road which runs from Szetlewek to Szymanowice to Jarocin — heaped.

The twenty-first mound to the left of the Szetlew or Jarocin road, beside the cross road which runs from Łomowo to Królikowo — likewise thirty pręty — heaped.

The twenty-second and twenty-third mounds to the right of that same Szetlew road — likewise thirty pręty — heaped.

The twenty-fourth mound to the left of the road — at equal distance — heaped.

The twenty-fifth wall mound to the left of the Szetlew or Jarocin road, beside the cross road which runs from Łazy past the Trąbczyn olędry through the Czarniawa drainage strip and across the road from Łomowo to Królikowo — thirty-five pręty — heaped.

The twenty-sixth mound to the right of that same Szetlew road — thirty pręty — heaped.

The twenty-seventh mound to the left of that same road, close to the Czarniawa drainage strip

— likewise thirty pręty — heaped.

The twenty-eighth mound to the right of that same road, above the very Czarniawa drainage strip [i.e., to the north of the drainage strip] — likewise thirty pręty — heaped.

The twenty-ninth mound to the right of that same road, above the drainage strip [i.e., to the north of it], beside the second road which runs from the Trąbczyn olędry to Królikowo — likewise thirty pręty — heaped.

The thirtieth wall mound to the right of the Szetlew road, above the drainage strip [i.e., to the north of it] — likewise thirty pręty — heaped.

The thirty-first mound to the right of the road.

The thirty-second mound to the left of the road, above the Czarniawa drainage strip [i.e., to the north of it] — heaped in equal measure.

The thirty-third mound to the left of that same Szetlew road, going east as all the others from the beginning, above that same drainage strip [i.e., to the north of it] — likewise thirty pręty — heaped.

The thirty-fourth mound to the right of that same road — thirty pręty — heaped.

The thirty-fifth mound to the left.

The thirty-sixth and thirty-seventh mounds to the right of the road — likewise thirty pręty — heaped.

The thirty-eighth mound to the left of that same road — thirty pręty — heaped.

The thirty-ninth corner mound — a large one, one for three — beside that same road from Szetlewek

Mały to Jarocin, to the left of the road, straight east, one staj from Czarny Bród — heaped; which marks the confluence or ground and pine forest of three estates: Łazy, Trąbczyn Wielki or [the deserted lands] Drzęzgi, and Biskupice or the deserted lands of Bystrzyce. Beside that newly heaped mound to the left is a similar old mound called Tomal, left intact; beside that newly heaped mound are three crosses newly cut on three pines, and two old ones — and beside the old mound stands a pine with a cross, felled. In all those mounds, apart from those heaped on the bogs with stakes, the following markers are placed: brick, slag, glass, and charcoal.

And all those mounds, from Studzienka and the tavern hills and bogs — or rather from the corner mound established there — all the way to that large corner mound just described called Tomal, in one and the same easterly direction — heaped. After those mounds had been heaped in such manner, the present commission court and likewise the Sub-chamberlain office orders that the Honourable Prusimski, Starost of Niszczewice, heir of Trąbczyn Wielki, Łazy, Osiny, and Nowa Wieś or

Trąbczyn — complying both with the Crown law and with the sentence of his court in the performance of the oath — shall swear this boundary line as run and assigned to him in this boundary as well between the above-mentioned estates — on the last same corner mound just heaped, together with six witnesses equal to him in birth, in the formula prescribed by the statute: "As I have rightly and justly made this present demarcation by mounds and this boundary line, and have taken nothing whatsoever from the other party's ground by this present demarcation and mounds, nor have I wronged my neighbour." For the proclamation of that oath and formula the loyal Court Summoner Bartłomiej of Trąbczyn, assisting this present act, is assigned.

The Honourable Prusimski, complying therewith by the order of his court and with the Crown law, performed that oath with six witnesses possessing their own estates: namely the Honourables and Honest Kasper, father, and Benedykt, his son, Kurowski, heirs of a portion of Kurowo; Józef Pawłowski; Jan Bagiński; Stefan Waliszewski; Piotr Makowiecki — on the image of Christ the Lord crucified, in the formula described above, on the said

last corner mound newly heaped beside the old Tomal, in the presence of many worthy and distinguished persons, together with the witnesses.

After that compliant performance, the present court, having acknowledged the Honourable Prusimski, Starost of Niszczewice's compliance in this case, wishes to hold and confirms this present demarcation, marked by mounds and other signs, as inviolable; and ordered the Court Summoner to proclaim it.

After the demarcation of that boundary — since the Honourable Prusimski, in the boundary begun from the said corner mound between Trąbczyn Wielki or the deserted lands Drzęzgi and Biskupice or the deserted lands of Bystrzyce, unable to be concluded due to excessive flooding, likewise has — as to that boundary also — the boundary line from the said corner mound as far as Czarny Bród assigned and adjudged to him.

The commission court therefore orders the said Kalisz Boundary Surveyor also in that boundary — beside the very Czarny Bród and the beaten Szetlew road to Jarocin — to stand guard over of the corner mound, to heap [a mound] in the presence of his court — without prejudice to the further continuation and resolution from this point of that boundary — at the future appointed date of the adjournment, and to that end adjourns its further act for

the resolution and conclusion of that boundary to the twenty-first day of May of the coming year 1776; and with all that contained therein, limits and prorogues the commission from the Commonwealth, with all the powers granted to it.

And thereupon, having proceeded to confront with the boundary lines and position of places the map delineated by the Most Illustrious Father Surveyor during the present commission and submitted to its court at the determination of the commission with his signature — having confronted it with the position and layout of places accurately and found it conforming and agreeing in all — and signed by the Most Honourable President with the consent of his whole court — it issues [the map] to the Honourable Prusimski. And nonetheless the present court orders the printed schedule of documents produced in its court — signed by the President — to be either inserted alongside the present decree or submitted to the acta for oblata.

After the conclusion of the demarcation of the above-mentioned estates and their boundaries, the present commission court — resolving its suspension, made above, concerning the transgressions and the revenues arising from the ground now bounded off, and likewise concerning the claims and the delivery of possession, with the Honourable Chełmski — first finds a sworn inquiry to be necessary, ordering the Honourable Prusimski to draw up the interrogatories in that matter, sign them, and submit them to his court for the hearing of witnesses; and to introduce into it witnesses not involved in this matter, credible, and — having first sworn the preceding oath of non-subornation — trustworthy. The Honourable Prusimski, complying with his court's sentence, drew up, signed, and submitted the interrogatories to his court.

And thereupon performed the oath in the customary formula of non-subornation of those witnesses in any improper manner — before his court, on the image of the Passion of Christ the Lord — before the introduction of the witnesses for the sworn inquiry. The court, having acknowledged his compliance with its sentence, proceeded to hear the sworn inquiry — and having first heard from each witness individually the oath for truthful testimony, conducted and heard those sworn inquiries in the customary manner. After hearing them, and having heard the further controversies of the parties introduced into its court — it proceeded to the reading of those sworn inquiries, both those just conducted by it, and the second conducted in 1728 by the Kalisz land court authority concerning the transgressions also then attempted. And having thoroughly informed itself from both those and from the boundary documents, and having read them as diligently as possible — as to the transgressions and the claims arising from the now demarcated ground — since it is shown both from the sworn inquiries and from the judicial site inspections, manifests, and protests repeatedly suspended and made by the predecessors of the Honourable Starost of Niszczewice: that the Honourable Chełmski and likewise his father — having raised the controversy over the stawisko or rather the empty marshes and the other pine forests and woodlands now demarcated

as proper Trąbczyn property — frequently sent their people violently onto those pine forests, and especially the Lusnia, Miąsykąt, and the pine beside them, and likewise onto the stawisko, and for several dozen years had not a little timber felled — partly to the glassworks, partly for other building needs, and at times gave it to others. And likewise onto the stawisko or rather empty marshes — belonging to Trąbczyn pursuant to the partitions of 1522 and 1693 and the old boundaries, now demarcated — they frequently and violently intruded, and having several times there made hay took it away, and forbade the people of Nowa Wieś and Osiny to use them; and frequently and violently ordered a weir or rather a dam — on another's ground, in the manner of a small weir — to be made on that stawisko, several times when it was broken by Trąbczyn; and by that dam made by force, and by the water held through it and through their own [dam], they frequently flooded not only that ground which the Honourable Chełmski was now taking in his boundary line, but also that which remained outside his boundary line, and likewise the meadows, pastures, and pine forests growing upon the empty stawisko as well as lying above it; and by that flooding dried out not a little timber, and caused significant damage in both the meadows and the pastures and the pine forests and woodlands — of which still not a little dry timber, especially on the stawisko, both fallen and standing, is present and found. And likewise committed other transgressions against the heirs and people of Trąbczyn. And likewise from twelve olędry, for four years the Honourable Chełmski collected annual cash rents,

specified item by item from each separately in the register submitted by the Honourable Prusimski to his court with his own signature and signed by the commission President — and used them in various ways and for various services; and with the right of propination at the inn on the highway, for those same four years took no small income from spirits and beer.

And moreover it is shown from the sworn inquiries now conducted that the Honourable Chełmski — having received the olędry and the inn through the delivery of possession — and moreover as will be expressed below, contrary to the statute law on boundaries and by an unlawful proceeding — when the Honourable Prusimski, obstructing at least that unlawful possession of the inn, had settled his own people in that inn, the Honourable Chełmski — proceeding in this against the law, and taking no step at law, but by force, in person, with several dozen men armed with firearms of various kinds, having ridden upon that inn — at once ordered shooting through the window into the inn; and a nobleman, the Honourable Krzeczkowski, was very nearly killed, and had his heel shot off; and moreover the inn was set on fire, and was barely saved after the flight of the men on the Honourable Chełmski's own side. During which attack the Honourable Chełmski himself cried out to beat, kill, and shoot; and likewise ordered all the items there in the inn, expressed in the register submitted to his court by the Honourable Starost of Niszczewice with his own signature and signed by the commission President, to be seized — and did not surrender them to the [municipal court] chancery.

Therefore the present commission court, weighing these unlawful acts as condemned by the Crown law and disturbing the peace of the nobility, and manifesting the insufficient regard for the Crown law — deferring to those Crown laws prohibiting also the forcible seizure and appropriation of one's own property greatly compounded in controversy of ground —

and moreover preventing similar excesses from being penalised: the Honourable Stanisław Chełmski, for those transgressions committed and attached to his own person and the person of the same — arising both from the ground transgression and from the violent attack and shooting and the burning of the inn and for the unlawful use of firearms [pro abusu bombardarum] — the personal penalties being set aside as to the person of his father, regularly owed though they are, extinguished with his death —

it finds and orders that the said Honourable Chełmski shall take his seat in the tower of the Konin municipal court twelve weeks from the time of the present decree, and shall continue that sitting for two weeks consecutively; and upon leaving the tower shall pay and count out — four starost's pledges also in respect of the offence committed by his father, and separately for the unlawful use of firearms three hundred grzywien — to the Honourable Prusimski or his authorised plenipotentiary in the Konin municipal court, under outlawry in case of non-compliance with that decree, to be followed by the manifest there lodged at that same court.

As to the damages caused by the flooding — of pine forests felled both to the glassworks and for buildings, and for fuel; and from the meadows partaken for a substantial time; and the damages caused to the people thereby; and likewise for the services had from the olędry — the present commission court finds it just that all this is owed from the said Honourable Chełmski, heir of Łukomia, to the Honourable Prusimski, Starost of Niszczewice, heir of Trąbczyn.

And although a far larger sum would be owed to the Honourable Prusimski for those claims, nonetheless the court, fixing no larger sum than eight thousand Polish złoty for the Honourable Prusimski by the moderation of its court —

and ordering the Honourable Starost of Niszczewice to swear that moderated sum without delay before his court, on the image of Christ the Lord crucified, in this formula: "that that sum is justly owed to him, as moderated by his court, for the felled pine forests and woodlands, and the drying out of various timber, and for other wrongs done to him" — and assigns to him the Court Summoner for the proclamation of that oath and formula.

The Honourable Prusimski swore in the presence of his court and many other persons. To that oath the present commission court orders that the said Honourable Chełmski shall pay and count out that sum, sworn by the Honourable Prusimski, Starost of Niszczewice, within eight weeks from the time and act of the present decree in the present year — under outlawry before that same authority and the Konin court records, with the manifest to be lodged there in case of non-compliance at that same appointed date — to the said Honourable Prusimski, Starost of Niszczewice, or his authorised plenipotentiary; [along with the] lawful penalty of fourteen grzywien to that same party, payable likewise at that same appointed date under similar outlawry.

As to the rent from the twelve olędry received in 1771 through the delivery of possession made in the Lusnia woodland on Monday after the Feast of All Saints and filed in the Konin municipal court on Monday after Saint Andrew the Apostle of the same year — since it is shown both from the sworn inquiry and from that same delivery of possession that the Honourable Chełmski received twelve olędry and the inn with stabling; and those olędry from the settler's rent — that is from the fields and meadows pursuant to law and statute — paid annual rent to the Honourable Starost of Niszczewice, their lord, of

six hundred and seventy Polish złoty and twenty-six grosze;

and that rent the Honourable Chełmski collected from them annually and punctually, and reckoning four years from the time of the delivery of possession to the present act — collected for four years the sum of two thousand six hundred and eighty-eight Polish złoty and fourteen grosze —

the present commission court, combining likewise the second sum of three thousand four hundred and eighty-six Polish złoty — reckoning only the profit from beer and spirits for propination, and likewise for the items seized in the inn during the violent attack and its seizure, paid to the noblemen — specified item by item in the register submitted by the Honourable Prusimski, Starost of Niszczewice, to his court with his own signature and signed by the President of the present commission —

both sums into one total sum of six thousand one hundred and sixty-nine Polish złoty and fourteen grosze — finds and adjudges it to be justly owed to the Honourable Prusimski, Starost of Niszczewice, from the Honourable Chełmski, by way of satisfaction, upon the hearing first of the oath therefor. And ordering the said Honourable Prusimski, Starost of Niszczewice, to swear that sum in the following formula: "as that sum is justly owed to him, as adjudged, for the seized rent from the olędry and the income from the inn, and for the items seized in that inn during the violent expulsion of the Honourable Prusimski" — without delay before his court, on the image of Christ the Lord crucified — assigns to him the Court Summoner. The Honourable Prusimski, Starost of Niszczewice, swore and likewise swore that sum.

After that oath the commission court, having found that sum to be genuine and just, orders that the Honourable Chełmski shall pay and count it out to the Honourable Prusimski or his authorised plenipotentiary in the present year

at the above-mentioned appointed date — together with the above sum — before that same authority and the Konin municipal court records, genuinely and punctually; under similar outlawry in case of non-compliance with the present decree, with the manifest lodged together for both sums at that same appointed date at that authority; [along with the] lawful penalty of fourteen grzywien payable to the said Honourable Prusimski at that same appointed date and place under similar outlawry.

As to the lawfulness of the delivery of possession and the proceeding — although the first default judgement of 1761 on Monday after Saint Bartholomew the Apostle, in the field, [obtained] on the late Honourable Antoni Prusimski, uncle of the present plaintiff — after the filed court appearance and withdrawal from it, with the hand-pledge adjournment made by him with the Honourables Chełmski brothers on the twenty-second April in Trąbczyn of that same year 1761, past the nephew who is the present plaintiff — was obtained at the instance of both Honourable Chełmski brothers, and is shown to have been filed in the Konin municipal court on Wednesday after Saint Matthew the Apostle of the same year. Yet since that default judgement was obtained after the decree had issued — contrary to the statute law on boundary causes, which orders all co-heirs of any estate whatsoever, not excepting also minors, to belong to the boundary cause and to respond in it — on the foundation of which law the late Honourable Antoni Prusimski, uncle of the present plaintiff, having — past his nephew who had never been in his guardianship, and moreover who at that time already had full age and was managing his own affairs — filed a manifest in the Konin municipal court on Wednesday after

Saint Bartholomew the Apostle of that same year 1761 — concerning the invalidity of the adjournment, and also that he cannot respond and be adjudged without his nephew and co-heir of Trąbczyn — defended himself with that manifest; and accordingly on the foundation of that manifest and likewise of the terminus attested by himself and his nephew in the Konin municipal court in the same year — sought referral to the Tribunal. And the field court, not heeding those defences and the law — past the nephew and without his court appearance — making him as though guardian of that nephew, ordered him alone to dispute with the Honourables and Honest Chełmski in the present cause, and by that unlawful sentence compelled him to withdraw his court appearance — and in such an unlawful manner was it obtained. And thereupon the present plaintiff, the Honourable Prusimski, proves and demonstrates to his court that he was never in that uncle's guardianship; and moreover proves by lease contracts concluded with the late Honourable Bińkowski and likewise by his birth certificate that he already at that time had full age and was managing his own affairs. Therefore the present commission court — weighing both the Crown statute law on boundaries and the above-mentioned proofs — finds the unlawful sentence of the above-mentioned field court and likewise that default judgement unlawfully obtained and issued against the late Honourable Antoni Prusimski, uncle of the present plaintiff — overturns it and quashes it without penalty. And for the same reasons and grounds releases the Honourable Starost of Niszczewice, nephew as successor of the same, from the penalty for contravention — insofar as it would fall half upon the person of the late Honourable Antoni Prusimski, uncle.

As to the other half of that contravention of the Tribunal decree — or rather the penalty therefor — since the present plaintiff, the Honourable Prusimski,

the nephew, did not register at the court appearance appointed date and thereby contravened the Tribunal decree — the present commission court, imposing upon him the half penalty for that contravention, adjudges one starost's pledge owed to the Honourable Chełmski from him.

As to the second default judgement — obtained in the Poznań Tribunal on Thursday after the Exaltation of the Holy Cross of 1767 against the present plaintiff, after the filed court appearance, at the instance of the Honourables Chełmski — since that was overturned by the Bydgoszcz Tribunal decree of Monday after the Feast of the Visitation of the Blessed Virgin Mary of 1768, and a half-double pledge was adjudged to the Honourable Chełmski for it — the court therefore adjudges that same half-double pledge owed to the Honourable Chełmski from the said Honourable Prusimski.

As to the contravention of the Tribunal decree of Tuesday on the day after the Feast of Saint Aegidius of 1766 — since the Honourable Prusimski acted against that decree by appealing it, and that decree was improved by the later Bydgoszcz Tribunal decree expressed above; nor did the Honourable Chełmski himself bring it to effect; and moreover since the later decree, resolving the default judgement expressed above, did not adjudge and resolve that contravention of the said decree — therefore the present commission court does not recognise that contravention and releases the present plaintiff, the Honourable Prusimski, from it as free.

As to the contravention of the last Bydgoszcz Tribunal decree

of 1768 expressed above of the present act, and likewise the last default judgement therefrom — rather from the adjournment — filed in the Kalisz municipal court on Monday after the Feast of the Eleven Thousand Virgins of 1770 — the present commission court, resolving that contravention and default judgement, orders that the Honourable Prusimski, Starost of Niszczewice, shall pay and count out to the Honourable Chełmski for that contravention of the said last decree and likewise for the default judgement obtained on the foundation of that decree — three starost's pledges together with the above-mentioned one and a half pledge adjudged by his court — at the appointed date for receiving the sums and grzywien adjudged to him from the Honourable Chełmski as above, before the Konin municipal court records and authority — to the said Honourable Chełmski, under outlawry upon the manifest lodged there in case of non-compliance with the present decree; or to deduct them from the sums and grzywien adjudged to the Honourable Chełmski.

As to the delivery of possession of the olędry and the grounds in controversy — made in the Lusnia woodland in 1771 on Monday after the Feast of All Saints by the Honourable Wawrzyniec Wstowski, Commissioner of the Poznań and Kalisz voivodeships, on behalf and in favour of the Honourable Stanisław Chełmski, heir of the Łukomia estate — filed in the Konin municipal court on Monday after Saint Andrew the Apostle of the same year.

Since that delivery of possession is shown not only to have been conducted by a bad proceeding resolved above by his court — and entirely only upon two default judgements — but also contrary to the statute law on

boundary causes; which law in boundary causes orders not delivery of possession upon a completed proceeding but the serving of citations from the Sub-chamberlain in the prescribed order for the absentee demarcation — and contrary to that law and custom; and moreover without the writ of execution established by new law — is shown to his court to be unlawful: not so much carried out officially as rather violently; and moreover conducted on another's ground and with another's people now demarcated by his court.

Therefore the court of the present commission, finding that delivery of possession to be unlawful, overturns, quashes, and annihilates it. And thereupon orders those olędry and the inn with stabling, and the grounds and pine forests surrendered to the Honourable Chełmski through that delivery of possession — insofar as they are now already demarcated and marked by mounds — to be immediately vacated and returned on behalf and in favour of the Honourable Prusimski. And rather, the court itself immediately places the Honourable Prusimski in possession — under outlawry in case of opposition to its court's judgements and obstruction of the Honourable Prusimski's possession within eight weeks from the time of the decree, to be determined at the Konin municipal court upon the manifest lodged there. And since the Honourable Chełmski and likewise the Honourable Wstowski, Commissioner of the Poznań and Kalisz voivodeships, unheard-of and not practised in a boundary cause — conducted such a delivery of possession in a woodland and rather violently than officially; and thereby transgressed and

violated the law both on boundaries and on writs of execution — the court therefore holds the Honourable Chełmski to be subject to penalty — both for himself and for his official. And although a larger penalty would be due pursuant to the new law, yet the court, reducing it and omitting the personal penalty, orders that the Honourable Chełmski — both for himself and standing for his official — shall pay to the Honourable Prusimski or his authorised plenipotentiary one thousand grzywien pursuant to the new law on boundaries — likewise within eight weeks, in the Konin municipal court in the present year — under outlawry at that same appointed date, upon a separate manifest lodged.

As to the pretended expulsion and penalty claimed by the Honourable Chełmski against the Honourable Prusimski — since that delivery of possession, as expressed above, was unlawfully conducted, and the Honourable Prusimski, defending his own property, only settled his own people in the inn — whom the Honourable Chełmski, as expressed above, was evident from the sworn inquiry, expelled — the court therefore, deferring to justice, releases from all penalty claimed by the Honourable Chełmski — both the Honourable Prusimski and his people.

As to the contravention of the Piotrków Tribunal decree of Thursday after Saint Martin of 1760 in this cause, and the default judgement after withdrawal from the court appearance — from that same field decree, obtained in the field on Monday after the Sunday Exaudi of the same year at the instance of the Honourable Prusimski against the Honourable Chełmski, filed in the Konin municipal court on Friday on the very feast of

Saint Anthony of Padua — since it is shown from that very field act expressed above that the Honourable Chełmski together with his brother, not having proved their convergence or corner mound, and indeed — having overturned the table on which the official was writing, as expressed in the field act — withdrew from the court appearance and submitted themselves as beaten;

and thereby contravened both the Tribunal decree and the field decree — the present commission court therefore orders that the Honourable Chełmski, discharging that default judgement, shall pay half-double pledge, and for the contravention the double pledge pursuant to law — at the above-mentioned appointed date at the Konin municipal court — to the Honourable Prusimski, together with the grzywien adjudged above, under similar outlawry in case of contravention.

As to the costs and expenses claimed by the Honourable Prusimski to be satisfied by the Honourable Chełmski — the present commission court, suspending its resolution of that claimed costs category, reserves it for adjudication at the above-mentioned appointed date arising from the adjournment in the boundary between Trąbczyn Wielki or Drzęzgi and Biskupice or the deserted lands of Bystrzyce. And since this act proceeds in a split court — the court, having sealed four sworn inquiries: one of 1728 concerning the stawisko, a second of 1760 concerning the corner mound, a third concerning the entire

boundary line of the Honourable Prusimski, and a fourth concerning the transgressions and claims, all conducted in the present year by its court — delivers and issues those sworn inquiries to the Honourable Prusimski, and likewise the map confronted and signed by the President.

Whereof this present decree gives satisfaction, by the force of the law or constitution enacted by the Estates of the Commonwealth.

Ludwik Dąmbski, Voivode of Brest Kuyavia, President of the commission, (signed in own hand) [place of the family seal]

